window.BH_SEARCH = [{"href":"docs/adr-001-rate-limit-in-each-service.html","title":"ADR-001: Rate limit inside each service","kind":"decision","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Architecture decision record ADR-001: Rate limit inside each service Each service enforces its own per-client token bucket in process, so we can ship limits before launch without new infrastructure. We will embed a per-client token bucket in every service, keyed by API key, because launch is three weeks away and this needs no shared infrastructure."},{"id":"context","heading":"Context","text":"The public API launches on 2 December. Two design partners have scripted clients that retry aggressively, and nothing today stops one of them saturating a service. We have no gateway able to run custom logic, and no shared low-latency store in production."},{"id":"options","heading":"Options considered","text":"In-process limiter per service Ships with a library and a config file No new runtime dependency Limits multiply with replicas and services Central limiter service One accurate limit per client New service, new on-call, before launch"},{"id":"consequences","heading":"Consequences","text":"What gets easier Each team tunes limits for its own endpoints What gets harder A client's real limit is per-service limit × replicas × services , so it grows as we scale Limits drift between services"}]},{"href":"docs/adr-002-rate-limit-at-the-edge.html","title":"ADR-002: Rate limit at the edge gateway","kind":"decision","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Architecture decision record ADR-002: Rate limit at the edge gateway Enforce one per-client token bucket in the gateway, with state in Redis, so limits hold however many services and replicas run behind it. We will enforce client rate limits once, in the gateway, using a token bucket updated atomically in Redis, because per-service limits no longer bound what a client can send. Services keep only a coarse safety limit against runaway internal callers."},{"id":"context","heading":"Context","text":"Under the per-service limits of ADR-001, one client sent 9× its contracted rate during the September incident without hitting a single limit: their traffic was spread across 3 services with 3 replicas each. We now have a gateway that runs plugins and a managed Redis cluster with p99 under 2 ms in-region. The token bucket explainer covers the algorithm, and the design document covers the request path, the script and the canary results."},{"id":"options","heading":"Options considered","text":"Keep per-service limits No migration The limit still multiplies as we scale Gateway + Redis token bucket One accurate limit per client Rejections never reach services Redis on the request path: +1–2 ms, needs a fail-open policy CDN provider limiting Absorbs floods before our network Can't see API keys or weight endpoints"},{"id":"consequences","heading":"Consequences","text":"What gets easier Limits are contractual numbers that hold One place to tune, observe and explain a 429 What gets harder Redis becomes part of every request; if it's down we fail open and lean on service safety limits"}]},{"href":"docs/announcing-patchcov.html","title":"Announcing patchcov","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Announcement · Testing Announcing patchcov patchcov answers one question precisely: which lines did this change add that no test ran? It is a command-line tool that reads the coverage report you already produce, and a GitHub Action that turns its answer into a pull-request comment and a gate. Neither needs an account, a token or a server. Command line & Rust library rust-works/patchcov Install it, run patchcov diff , read the docs GitHub Action action-works/patchcov-action Comment and gate on every pull request patchcov scores the lines a change added , lists the untested ones as file:line , and exits non-zero when a gate fails. patchcov-action runs it on every pull request, with a baseline from the merge base. That output is meant for automation as much as for people. An AI agent can loop on it, \"add tests until the gate passes\", without reading a dashboard. The project-wide percentage is the wrong signal for a pull request. A change can leave a third of its new code untested and barely move the total."},{"id":"motivation","heading":"Why another coverage tool","text":"More and more code is written by agents in a loop: write, run the checks, read the result, fix, repeat. That loop is only as good as the signals it can read. Coverage tooling was built to give people a picture, and an agent needs an instruction. Three things kept getting in the way: The total hides the change. A 4,000-line project at 92% looks the same after a pull request that adds six lines and tests four. The new code's gap is real, but the total is too coarse to show it. Dashboards need interpreting. Trend graphs and coloured file trees are made for a person to look at. A script or an agent needs a number, a list of lines, and an exit code. A hosted service is a dependency of your merge button. If the coverage gate lives on someone else's server, an outage, an expired upload token or a rate limit blocks merges. Nothing can be checked before you push, either. patchcov is the smallest tool that fixes all three. It reads report files from disk, joins them to git diff , and prints a result that needs no human to interpret it. The mission, from the README: help projects that use agentic workflows keep their new code properly tested, with minimal friction. Predict: a project has 4,000 executable lines at 92.00%. A pull request adds 6 executable lines and tests 4 of them. How far does the project total move? From 92.00% to 91.96%, a move of −0.04 percentage points. (3,680 + 4) ÷ (4,000 + 6) = 91.96%. That is under the 0.05 pp tolerance patchcov uses for its headline delta, so the total renders as neutral ⚪. The patch coverage for the same change is 66.67% , with two lines named. Try it below."},{"id":"patch-coverage","heading":"Patch coverage, hands on","text":"Patch coverage is the share of the added executable lines that some test ran. Click a line's mark to decide whether a test covers it, and watch both numbers. 40 pub fn parse_header(line: &str) -> Result<Header, Error> { 41 let line = line.trim(); 42 + // A shebang is not a header. 43 + ✓ if line.starts_with(\"#!\") { 44 + ✓ return Ok(Header::Shebang); 45 + } 46 + ✓ let Some((key, value)) = line.split_once(':') else { 47 + ✗ return Err(Error::MissingColon); 48 + }; 49 + ✓ if key.is_empty() { 50 + ✗ return Err(Error::EmptyKey); 51 + } 52 Ok(Header::Field(key.into(), value.trim().into())) 53 } Lines 42, 45, 48 and 51 are added but not executable: a comment and closing braces never appear in a coverage report, so they are not counted. 66.67% Patch coverage (4/6 new lines) 91.96% Project total ⚪ −0.04 pp, neutral --fail-under-patch 80 Patch: 66.67% (4/6 new lines covered) Uncovered new lines: src/parser.rs:47, src/parser.rs:50 Error: patch coverage 66.67% is below the --fail-under-patch threshold of 80.00% exit 1 Loop until the gate passes Add one test Reset The total barely moves however many lines you leave untested; patch coverage swings by 16.67 points per line and names each gap. “Loop” plays the agent’s part: it writes a test for the first line patchcov lists, runs the gate again, and stops when it exits 0."},{"id":"how-it-works","heading":"How patchcov works","text":"patchcov is a join between two things you already have: a per-line coverage report, and the lines git says the change added. Notice that nothing leaves the machine: every input is a file or a git object, and every output is stdout or an exit code. Read the report you already have. lcov, llvm-cov JSON, Cobertura, JaCoCo XML and Go coverprofiles are detected from the content, so one command serves Rust, Go, Java, Kotlin, TypeScript, Python, C++ and more. Paths are made repo-relative by a fixed, configurable pipeline, with no guessing: a wrong guess would attribute coverage to the wrong file without a sign. Ask git what the change added. By default that is everything from the merge base with the default branch to HEAD , so lines that reached main while your branch was open are not counted against you. Score each added line. It is covered, uncovered, or not executable. Lines the report doesn't mention, such as comments and braces, are left out of the denominator. Optionally, compare with a baseline. A report from the merge base adds per-file deltas and indirect changes: lines whose coverage flipped without their text changing. These are scoped to the files the diff touched, because two separate test runs always differ a little elsewhere. Print, then exit. The markdown is a ready-made pull-request comment; JSON and YAML follow a documented schema. The exit code is 0 , or 1 when --fail-under-patch or --fail-under-lines fails, and 2 – 8 for a usage, report, marker, config, git or path problem. So a script can tell a failed gate from a broken run."},{"id":"agent-loop","heading":"Built for the agent loop","text":"Because the answer is a list of file:line and an exit code, an agent can act on it without a person in between. Agent Test run patchcov tests + coverage head.lcov diff --fail-under-patch 80 exit 1 · parser.rs:47, :50 write 2 tests tests + coverage diff --fail-under-patch 80 exit 0 The agent never reads a chart. Each round trip is a command, a list of lines, and a yes or no. The agent runs the test suite under any coverage tool that writes a supported report, such as cargo llvm-cov , go test -coverprofile , coverage.py or JaCoCo. patchcov exits 1 and names the gaps. With -o json , they arrive as uncovered_new_lines , and --error-format json puts the cause on stderr as an object too. The agent writes tests for exactly those lines and runs both steps again. The same command works on a laptop, in a sandbox and in CI, so the agent can pass the gate before it pushes."},{"id":"trustworthy","heading":"A signal you can trust","text":"A gate that cries wolf gets ignored, by people and agents alike. patchcov is quiet where coverage is noisy and loud where it is broken. 1 Quiet by construction Deltas are scoped to the files the diff touched. The headline ignores moves under 0.05 pp. Code that flaps between runners, such as a path gated on a CPU feature, can be wrapped in a tolerate marker, so a flap isn't reported as a regression. 2 Fails loudly An empty report, a failed shard, report paths that match no tracked file, or a malformed marker is an error with its own exit code, not a quietly lower number. 3 Silencing is visible Every marker needs a reason= , and every exclusion is listed in the comment. Settings live in .patchcov/config.yaml , in version control, so a reviewer can see what the gate does. // patchcov: coverage tolerate reason=\"AVX2 path; runners differ in CPU\" fn popcount_avx2(words: &[u64]) -> u64 { /* … */ } // patchcov: coverage end Go deeper: why tolerated lines still count in the patch A tolerate region masks deltas : each tolerated line is scored with its status in the baseline, so a flap reads as no change. The displayed percentages stay the real, measured values. An added line has no baseline counterpart, so it keeps its real status and stays in the patch-coverage denominator: new code should still be tested, even if it flaps later. To remove lines from every number, use ignore instead."},{"id":"action","heading":"patchcov-action: the same answer on every pull request","text":"The command answers the question; the action wires it into GitHub. It installs a cached patchcov binary, finds the right baseline, posts one sticky comment, and applies the gates last. Notice that the baseline is pinned to the pull request’s fork point, not to the tip of main , so the deltas belong to this pull request alone. Every push to main publishes a baseline: that commit's per-line report, as an artifact named coverage-baseline . On a pull request, produce the head report. In fat mode (the default) the action runs cargo-llvm-cov for a Rust workspace. In thin mode you bring a report from any language, and the action only diffs, comments and gates. Fetch the baseline for the merge base. If that commit has none, it tries up to 10 first-parent ancestors, nearest first, and says so in the comment. As a last resort in fat mode, it rebuilds coverage at the merge base in a git worktree. A miss is a warning: patch coverage never needs a baseline. Post one comment and keep it current. It has the patch coverage, the per-file deltas and the uncovered file:line list, and is updated in place on every push. Gate last. fail-under-patch and fail-under-lines run after the comment posts, so a failing pull request still gets its explanation. The action also handles the topologies real projects grow into: sharded test runs merged into one result, merge queues, and files a CI runner can't execute. jobs: coverage: runs-on: ubuntu-latest permissions: contents: read actions: read # download the merge base's baseline pull-requests: write # post the coverage comment steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v7 with: fetch-depth: 0 # full history, so the merge base resolves - uses: action-works/patchcov-action@v2 with: fail-under-patch: 80 - run: pytest --cov --cov-report=lcov:coverage.lcov - uses: action-works/patchcov-action@v2 with: run-coverage: false report: coverage.lcov fail-under-patch: 80 coverage: needs: shard # a failed shard fails this job steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v7 with: fetch-depth: 0 - uses: actions/download-artifact@v8 with: pattern: coverage-shard-* merge-multiple: true path: shards - uses: action-works/patchcov-action@v2 with: run-coverage: false shard-reports: shards/shard-*.lcov fail-under-patch: 80 cargo install patchcov --locked # or: cargo binstall patchcov cargo llvm-cov --lcov --output-path head.lcov patchcov diff --report head.lcov --fail-under-patch 80"},{"id":"when-to-choose","heading":"When to choose patchcov","text":"patchcov is a signal, not a dashboard. Pick it for the question it answers, and keep the other tools for the questions they answer. patchcov A command and an action that score the change, in your own CI. Answers \"what did this change leave untested, and where?\" Machine-readable output and distinct exit codes, ready for agents No service, account or upload token: the gate works whenever your CI does Runs the same locally, in a sandbox and in CI Built for noisy coverage and sharded runs No history, trend graphs or cross-repository views Line coverage; branch scoring is opt-in and limited to lcov and Cobertura A hosted coverage service You upload reports; the service keeps them and comments on pull requests. Coverage history and trends over months Dashboards and views across many repositories A third party between your tests and your merge button Reports leave your CI; tokens to manage The result is learned after pushing, not before A code-quality platform Coverage as one input to a wider quality gate. Static analysis, security scanning and coverage in one place Another server to run or to depend on Much more to set up than a coverage gate needs Choose by the question you need answered. If you need… Choose To know whether a pull request added untested code, and where patchcov A gate an agent or script can act on, in a loop patchcov A coverage gate that can't be blocked by someone else's outage patchcov One combined result from a sharded test run patchcov Coverage history, trend graphs, or views across repositories A hosted service, alongside patchcov Per-branch percentages, or JaCoCo and llvm-cov branch data Your coverage tool's own report Static analysis and security scanning as well A code-quality platform You don't have to pick one patchcov-action can upload to Codecov as well ( codecov: true ), so the history keeps flowing to a dashboard while the gate stays in your own CI."},{"id":"limits","heading":"What it doesn't tell you","text":"Covered is not tested patchcov measures that a line ran , not that a test asserted anything about it. Treat it as a regression signal, and pair it with review, or with mutation testing, for confidence in the tests themselves. Measure the report at the revision you diff. A report from older code has line numbers that no longer match the diff, and the result is silently wrong. Check out full history in CI ( fetch-depth: 0 ), or the merge base won't resolve. The library API is not stable at 0.x. The command line is the better-supported interface."},{"id":"get-started","heading":"Get started","text":"Install. cargo install patchcov --locked , cargo binstall patchcov , or a prebuilt binary for Linux, macOS or Windows from the GitHub releases. Run it on your branch. Produce a per-line report from your test run, then patchcov diff --report head.lcov . Add the gate. Add action-works/patchcov-action@v2 to your pull-request workflow with fail-under-patch , and let the comment and the exit code do the rest. Command line & Rust library rust-works/patchcov Install it, run patchcov diff , read the docs GitHub Action action-works/patchcov-action Comment and gate on every pull request"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"rust-works/patchcov : the command, its usage guide, the reference for every flag and the JSON schema. Explanation : why patchcov's total differs from llvm-cov's summary, and how scoping and tolerate masking work. action-works/patchcov-action : fat and thin modes, sharded runs, merge queues and every input. docs.rs/patchcov : the analysis as a Rust library."}]},{"href":"docs/component-gallery.html","title":"Component gallery","kind":"reference","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Reference Component gallery Every building block in the theme, rendered live. View source to copy the markup — each section is self-contained. Pages are plain HTML linking theme/bh.css and theme/bh.js — no build step. Chrome (top bar, contents, progress, anchors, theme toggle) is added automatically. Interactive parts are web components that fall back to readable static content."},{"id":"typography","heading":"Typography","text":"A .lead paragraph opens a section with a little more presence. Body copy is set in IBM Plex Sans at a comfortable measure, with headings in Fraunces. Inline elements: inline_code() , ⌘ K , highlighted text , ADR , and a link . Sidenotes keep asides out of the main line of thought. Hover, focus or tap the number to read it: it opens in the same popup as a glossary term."},{"id":"headings","heading":"A third-level heading","text":"Second-level headings get an automatic §n eyebrow and appear in the contents; third-level headings are indented beneath them."},{"id":"callouts","heading":"Callouts","text":"Note Neutral information the reader should not miss. Tip A shortcut or recommended practice. Gotcha Something that surprises people. Danger Data loss, security, or outage risk."},{"id":"badges","heading":"Badges","text":"Draft Proposed Accepted In review Rejected Superseded High Medium Low v2.4.0"},{"id":"cards-metrics","heading":"Cards & metrics","text":"1 Single source Tokens in one file drive every colour, size and duration. 2 Both themes Light and dark from light-dark() — no duplicated rules. 3 Degrades well No JavaScript? Steps become a list, tabs become sections. 99.95% Availability (30d) 142 ms p99 latency +18% 3.2k Requests / s +6% 0 Open incidents"},{"id":"charts","heading":"Charts","text":"Write the numbers as a table inside <bh-chart> to get a themed line or column chart. Hover, or focus the chart and use the arrow keys, to read every series at a point. The table stays one click away under “Show data”. Day Before After Mon 412 402 Tue 398 260 Wed 430 190 Thu 405 176 Fri 441 181 Sat 390 172 Sun 420 168 Tail latency fell by more than half once the edge limiter shed abusive bursts. emphasis puts the story in colour and the context in grey. Region Per service At the edge us-east 4.1 1.2 eu-west 3.6 1.1 ap-south 6.8 1.9 sa-east 2.2 0.8 Column charts group series per category. Only the extreme value is labelled; the axis, tooltip and table carry the rest."},{"id":"comparisons","heading":"Comparisons & decisions","text":"PostgreSQL Relational, mature, already operated by the team. Transactions across tables Existing runbooks Vertical scaling ceiling DynamoDB Managed key-value with predictable latency. No capacity planning Access patterns fixed up-front Vendor lock-in We will use PostgreSQL with read replicas, because transactional integrity outweighs elastic scaling at our current size. Revisit when write volume exceeds 5k/s. When the choice depends on how much each criterion matters, show the scoring with <bh-matrix> . Readers can move the weights and watch whether the leader changes. Scores 1–5, higher is better. Weights 0–5 say how much each criterion matters. Criterion Weight PostgreSQL DynamoDB CockroachDB Multi-table transactions 4 5 2 5 Team familiarity 3 5 2 2 Elastic scaling 2 3 5 4 Cost at current scale 3 4 3 2 Vendor independence 2 5 1 4"},{"id":"sequences","heading":"Steps & timelines","text":"Drain the node. Stop routing new traffic and wait for in-flight requests. Upgrade. Apply the new image and restart the service. Verify & restore. Health checks pass, then re-add to the pool. 14:02 Deploy begins Config change rolled to 5% of hosts. 14:06 Error rate spikes 5xx climbs to 12% on canary hosts. 14:09 Alert fires On-call paged by SLO burn alert. 14:15 Rolled back Error rate returns to baseline."},{"id":"data","heading":"Key-values & tables","text":"Service billing-api Owner Payments platform Runtime Rust 1.90, Tokio, Axum Latency by region, last 7 days. Region p50 p99 Meets SLO us-east-1 21 ms 96 ms Yes eu-west-1 24 ms 131 ms Yes ap-southeast-2 38 ms 412 ms No"},{"id":"disclosure","heading":"Disclosure & predict-then-reveal","text":"Go deeper: why not just retry? Retries multiply load during an outage. Without jittered backoff and a retry budget, a brief blip becomes a sustained overload. If each of 3 layers retries 3 times, how many calls can one request cause? 4³ = 64. Each layer makes up to 4 attempts (1 + 3 retries), and they multiply."},{"id":"code","heading":"Code","text":"Language classes get syntax highlighting (Prism, loaded only when needed). Every block gets a copy button; data-title labels it. pub fn try_acquire(&mut self, now: Instant) -> bool { self.refill(now); // ① if self.tokens >= 1.0 { self.tokens -= 1.0; // ② true } else { false // ③ } }"},{"id":"annotated-code","heading":"Annotated code","text":"let tokens = min(capacity, tokens + elapsed * rate); 1 if tokens >= cost { tokens -= cost; allow() } 2 else { reject(retry_after(cost - tokens)) } 3 Refill lazily from elapsed time — no background timer needed. Spend tokens only when the request is admitted. Tell the client exactly when to come back."},{"id":"diffs","heading":"Diffs","text":"timeout_ms: 2000 - retries: 5 + retries: 2 + retry_budget: 0.1 backoff: exponential"},{"id":"code-walkthrough","heading":"Code walkthrough","text":"For explaining an implementation, <bh-codewalk> pins the code beside the prose. As you scroll, each step highlights its lines and shades the rest. use std::time::{Duration, Instant}; pub struct TokenBucket { capacity: f64, // b: the largest burst rate: f64, // r: tokens per second tokens: f64, last: Instant, // monotonic clock } impl TokenBucket { pub fn try_acquire(&mut self, cost: f64) -> Result<(), Duration> { let now = Instant::now(); let elapsed = now.duration_since(self.last).as_secs_f64(); self.tokens = (self.tokens + elapsed * self.rate).min(self.capacity); self.last = now; if self.tokens >= cost { self.tokens -= cost; Ok(()) } else { Err(Duration::from_secs_f64((cost - self.tokens) / self.rate)) } } } The whole state is four fields. Capacity and rate are configuration; tokens and last are all the limiter remembers per client. Refill lazily. No timer runs in the background: on each call, add the tokens earned since last and clamp to capacity. Admit by spending tokens. Weighted requests just pass a larger cost . Reject with a time. The error carries exactly how long until enough tokens exist, which becomes the Retry-After header."},{"id":"diagrams","heading":"Diagrams","text":"Inline SVG drawn with theme classes — .node.c1…c6 , .edge , .zone , .label . Colours follow the theme; arrowheads are automatic. Pair a diagram with <bh-stepper for=\"…\"> to walk through it. Each step is linkable ( #gallery-arch-step-3 ), and the link button in the stepper bar copies a link to the current step. A request path. Use the stepper below; edges animate while lit. External Network edge Our service Storage Infrastructure Prose can point into a diagram. Hover over the gateway , or over the service and its two stores , to light those parts. Click one to bring the diagram into view. Mark up the phrase with data-ref=\"figure-id:part-id …\" . The client calls the gateway over HTTPS; TLS terminates here. The gateway authenticates and forwards to the service . The service checks the cache first. On a miss it reads the database and back-fills the cache. Side effects are published to the queue asynchronously."},{"id":"current-vs-proposed","heading":"Current vs proposed","text":"Wrap two versions of a diagram in <bh-versions> to switch between them, or press Compare and drag the divider. Mark parts with data-change=\"added|changed|removed\" to show what the proposal changes. Every service enforces its own limit, so a client's real limit grows with the number of services. One limiter at the gateway sees all of a client's traffic, so the limit holds however many services there are."},{"id":"sequence-diagrams","heading":"Sequence diagrams","text":"Describe actors and messages, and <bh-sequence> lays them out with the theme's vocabulary. Replies are dashed, notes sit over one or more lifelines, and every part keeps its data-id , so a stepper can walk through the exchange. Client Limiter Service GET /orders refill tokens take 1 token GET /orders 200 OK later: bucket empty GET /orders 429 · Retry-After: 0.5 The limiter answers rejected requests itself; the service never sees them. The client calls the limiter. The limiter refills lazily, then takes a token. Admitted: the request reaches the service, which replies. When the bucket is empty, the limiter replies 429 without bothering the service."},{"id":"mermaid","heading":"Mermaid (fallback)","text":"For quick sequence or state diagrams, a <pre class=\"mermaid\"> renders with theme colours (loaded from a CDN only when present). Prefer hand-placed SVG for anything central to the argument. sequenceDiagram participant C as Client participant L as Limiter participant S as Service C->>L: request alt token available L->>S: forward S-->>C: 200 OK else bucket empty L-->>C: 429 Retry-After end"},{"id":"tabs","heading":"Tabs","text":"let resp = client.get(url).send().await?; resp, err := http.Get(url) resp = httpx.get(url)"},{"id":"playground","heading":"Playground","text":"Named inputs feed data-expr outputs and data-bind attributes — live models with no custom script. Replicas Per-replica capacity (rps) Offered load (rps) Utilisation % · headroom rps"},{"id":"glossary-terms","heading":"Glossary terms","text":"Words defined in the glossary are underlined automatically the first time they appear on a page. Hover over one to see its definition. For example, a gateway might enforce a rate limit with a token bucket and answer rejected calls with Retry-After, keeping p99 latency inside the SLO. The popup stays put while it's open. Move the pointer into it to select and copy text or follow its links. Click a term, or press Enter on it, to pin the popup; Esc or a click elsewhere closes it. To mark a word explicitly, use data-term=\"id\" , as in this GCRA reference . Add class=\"no-glossary\" to keep a passage unmarked."},{"id":"layout","heading":"Layout","text":"Children of .bh-article sit in the reading column. Add .wide to use the gutters, or .full to go edge to edge. .wide — figures, tables, comparisons .full — hero visuals, big diagrams"}]},{"href":"docs/deflate-format-reference.html","title":"DEFLATE format reference","kind":"reference","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Reference · Compression DEFLATE format reference Every range, field and bit-order rule in a DEFLATE stream, in one place, for reading a stream by hand or writing a decoder. The two explainers in this series say why the format is built this way; this page says exactly what it is. A series in three parts How DEFLATE compresses : the two savings, how they join, and a block read bit by bit. How a DEFLATE block sends its own code : codes fitted to the data, and how a block describes them cheaply. DEFLATE format reference , this page: every range, field and bit-order rule, for reading a stream or writing a decoder."},{"id":"two-alphabets","heading":"Every range, and the decoder's loop","text":"How LZ77's output maps onto DEFLATE's two alphabets, with the exact ranges. Part 1's join explains why lengths and distances become a range symbol plus plain extra bits. How LZ77's output maps onto DEFLATE's two alphabets. Where a symbol covers a range, plain extra bits follow it to pick the exact value; they are not Huffman-coded. LZ77 gives Symbol Alphabet Extra bits In the example a literal byte, 0–255 0–255, the byte's own value literal/length 0 a (byte 97) → 97 the end of the block 256 literal/length 0 256 , once at the end a length, 3–10 257–264, one each literal/length 0 length 4 → 258 a length, 11–257 265–284, each covering 2–32 lengths literal/length 1–5 length 23 → 270 ( 23–26 ) + 2 bits a length of 258 285 literal/length 0 a distance, 1–4 d0–d3, one each distance 0 a distance, 5–32,768 d4–d29, each covering 2–8,192 distances distance 1–13 distance 7 → d5 ( 7–8 ) + 1 bit; 12 → d6 ( 9–12 ) + 2 bits The decoder's inner loop runs the join backwards. Its two tables are the two alphabets: while True: sym = read_code(lit_len_table) # literal, length or end if sym < 256: out.append(sym) # a literal byte elif sym == 256: break # end of block else: i = sym - 257 length = LENGTH_BASE[i] + read_bits(LENGTH_EXTRA[i]) d = read_code(distance_table) # always follows a length distance = DIST_BASE[d] + read_bits(DIST_EXTRA[d]) for _ in range(length): # byte by byte: may overlap out.append(out[-distance])"},{"id":"not-symbols","heading":"Everything in the stream that isn't a symbol","text":"Parts 1 and 2 introduce each part of a stream where it is needed. To read a stream yourself, or to write a decoder, you need them in one place, with one rule for how each is written. A DEFLATE stream is mostly Huffman codes, but not entirely. Bits are written in two ways, and a decoder must know which kind comes next: A Huffman code is read one bit at a time, most significant bit first, because the decoder walks down the code tree as each bit arrives. Everything else is a plain number of a fixed, known width, read lowest bit first. Each byte of the stream fills from its lowest bit up, so a plain number's bits arrive in the order they are packed. In other words, DEFLATE is little-endian throughout, at the level of bits and of bytes (a stored block's 16-bit LEN comes low byte first), except that Huffman codes are stored bit-reversed. Write a byte the usual way, most significant bit on the left, and its plain fields read as ordinary binary, stacked from the right. Here is the first byte of part 2's dynamic block : 0 1 1 1 0 | 1 0 | 1 HLIT | BTYPE | BFINAL = 14 | = 2 | = 1 A Huffman code reads correctly in arrival order instead, so in this byte view it would look reversed. Decoders such as zlib reverse each code once, when they build their lookup tables, so that one little-endian bit reader can handle everything. Every plain field in a DEFLATE stream. None of these is Huffman-coded: the decoder knows each one's width from what came before it. Field Bits Where Holds BFINAL 1 start of every block 1 if this is the last block BTYPE 2 after BFINAL the block type ( values ) length extra bits 0–5 after a length symbol which length within the symbol's range: 270 means lengths 23–26, and its 2 extra bits, 0–3, add the offset ( try one ) distance extra bits 0–13 after a distance symbol which distance within the symbol's range: d6 means distances 9–12, and its 2 extra bits add the offset, so 11 = 3 gives 12 HLIT 5 dynamic block, after BTYPE the number of literal/length code lengths, minus 257: 257–288 is 32 values HDIST 5 after HLIT the number of distance code lengths, minus 1: 1–32 is 32 values HCLEN 4 after HDIST the number of code-length-code lengths, minus 4: 4–19 is 16 values code-length-code lengths 3 each after HCLEN , 4–19 of them the lengths of the code that compresses the code lengths, in the fixed order; that code's lengths are at most 7 run extra bits 2, 3 or 7 after code-length symbol 16, 17 or 18 the run's count, minus 3, 3 or 11 alignment 0–7 stored block, after BTYPE nothing: skipped to reach the next byte boundary LEN , NLEN 16 each stored block, after the alignment the number of bytes, then its ones' complement as a check stored bytes 8 each stored block, after NLEN the data itself, byte for byte final padding 0–7 after the last block nothing: fills the last byte Everything else in the stream is a Huffman code: the literal/length and distance symbols, and, inside a dynamic block's header, the code-length symbols. The worked example's 103 bits show both kinds side by side in the bit walk . Nothing in the stream says how long a field is. The decoder always knows what comes next and how big it is, in one of three ways: A width fixed by the RFC , chosen to fit the field's range: BFINAL , BTYPE , HLIT , HDIST , HCLEN , the 3-bit code-length-code lengths, and a stored block's LEN and NLEN . After BTYPE = 2, the next 5 bits are HLIT because the format says so. A width set by the symbol just decoded : the extra bits. Symbol 270 is always followed by 2 extra bits, d6 by 2, run symbol 18 by 7. No width at all : Huffman codes end themselves. The decoder reads one bit at a time until the bits so far match a code, and since no code is the start of another, it can never stop in the wrong place. That is why the stream needs no separators or length prefixes, and also why it must be read strictly in order: where HDIST starts depends on having read HLIT , and one corrupted bit misaligns everything after it."},{"id":"edge-cases","heading":"Edge cases","text":"Overlapping copies Allowed and common: distance 1 with length 258 repeats the previous byte 258 times. A decoder must copy byte by byte, not with a block copy, or it reads bytes that haven't been written yet. The longest match 258 bytes, with its own symbol (285) and no extra bits, so with the fixed codes a long run costs 13 bits per 258 bytes. 258 = 255 + 3: a length fits in a byte once the minimum of 3 is subtracted. End of block Symbol 256 ends every Huffman block, even the last. It needs a code, so it counts as a symbol when the tree is built. Empty input zlib writes 2 bytes, 03 00 : a final fixed block holding only the end-of-block code. Incompressible input Stored blocks cap the overhead at 5 bytes per 65,535. A large file of random bytes grows by under 0.01%; the 1 KiB in the chart grows by 0.5%."},{"id":"terms","heading":"Terms used on this page","text":"These are defined here, for this series. DEFLATE's broader terms are in the site glossary : DEFLATE , LZ77 , Huffman code , canonical Huffman code , back-reference , sliding window , end-of-block and extra bits . Alphabet The fixed set of symbols a Huffman code is built over. DEFLATE uses three: literal/length, distance and code-length. How the two ideas join BFINAL The first bit of every block: 1 if it is the last block of the stream. Blocks, and the fixed code BTYPE The 2 bits after BFINAL that give the block type: 00 stored, 01 fixed Huffman codes, 10 dynamic Huffman codes. 11 is invalid. Like every plain number, they arrive lowest bit first. The values Code length The number of bits in a symbol's Huffman code. 0 means the symbol has no code. A dynamic block sends only the lengths; the codes follow from them by the canonical rule. Send lengths, not trees Code-length code The small Huffman code a dynamic block uses to write down its code lengths. Its alphabet has 19 symbols: 0–15 are lengths, 16 repeats the previous length, and 17 and 18 are runs of zeros. CL is short for code length. Huffman-code the run symbols Distance alphabet The 30 symbols, d0–d29, that stand for back-reference distances from 1 to 32,768. Most stand for a range, picked out by extra bits. It has a Huffman code of its own. How the two ideas join Dynamic Huffman code Codes built for one block from that block's own symbol counts, and sent as code lengths at its start ( BTYPE 10). They don't change while the block is read: dynamic means per block, not adaptive. A dynamic block, bit by bit Fixed Huffman code Codes set once by the RFC, for any block with BTYPE 01, so no table is sent: 8 or 9 bits per literal, 7 or 8 per length symbol, 5 per distance symbol. Why 7–9 bits HCLEN 4 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many code-length-code lengths follow, minus 4. Those lengths come in a fixed order, so the count can cut off the usually-unused ones at the end. Why that order HDIST 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many distance code lengths follow, minus 1. In the bit walk HLIT 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many literal/length code lengths follow, minus 257. The lengths come as a bare list in symbol order, so the count says where the list ends. In the bit walk Literal A byte that LZ77 sends as itself, because no match of 3 or more starts there. It becomes the literal/length symbol with the same value, 0–255. LZ77 Literal/length alphabet The 286 symbols 0–285: 0–255 for literal bytes, 256 for the end of the block, and 257–285 for ranges of lengths. One Huffman code covers them all, so a single code says whether a literal or a copy comes next. How the two ideas join Overlapping copy A back-reference whose length is greater than its distance, so it reads bytes it is itself writing. It works because the decoder copies one byte at a time; distance 1 simply repeats a byte. The bit walk shows one Plain number A field of fixed, known width that is not Huffman-coded, read lowest bit first: the block header, the extra bits, a dynamic block's counts and code-length-code lengths, and a stored block's LEN and NLEN . Every plain field Prefix code A code in which no codeword is the start of another, so codes can be read back to back without separators. Every Huffman code is a prefix code. Huffman Stored block A block of up to 65,535 raw bytes ( BTYPE 00), after a byte-aligned length and its complement. Encoders use it when compressing would make the data bigger. Choosing a block type Symbol One element of an alphabet: what Huffman coding counts and gives a code. DEFLATE maps LZ77's tokens onto symbols, so Huffman coding never sees the raw bytes. How the two ideas join Token One item of LZ77's output: a literal, or a back-reference (length, distance). Each becomes one or two symbols. LZ77"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"RFC 1951, DEFLATE : the format itself, short and readable. Section 3.2 has the alphabets and the canonical code. RFC 1952, gzip and RFC 1950, zlib : the two wrappers, a few header bytes and a checksum around the same stream. Huffman (1952), \"A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes\" : the original paper. Ziv and Lempel (1977), \"A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression\" : where LZ77 gets its name. zlib's algorithm.txt : how zlib's encoder chooses matches, which this page leaves out."}]},{"href":"docs/edge-rate-limiting-design.html","title":"Moving rate limiting to the edge","kind":"design","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Design document Moving rate limiting to the edge Replace per-service limiters with one token bucket per client in the gateway, backed by Redis, so a client's limit holds however far we scale. Problem: per-service limits multiply with services and replicas. One client sent 9× its contract without being limited. Proposal: enforce limits once, in the gateway, with an atomic Redis script. Services keep only a coarse safety limit. Ask: review the fail-open policy and the rollout by 17 October."},{"id":"context","heading":"Context & problem","text":"Since ADR-001 , each service has run its own token bucket. That shipped quickly, but a limit enforced in nine places isn't a limit. During the September incident, one client's traffic spread across three services with three replicas each, and every replica admitted it. 9× Peak traffic vs contract, never limited 3 × 3 Services × replicas the limit was split across 412 ms p99 for everyone else during the incident"},{"id":"goals","heading":"Goals & non-goals","text":"Goals One limit per client, accurate to within 5% Rejected requests never reach a service Under 2 ms added at p99 Non-goals DDoS protection: that stays with the CDN Per-endpoint quotas (a later phase)"},{"id":"design","heading":"Proposed design","text":"Compare the two versions below. Today the limiters sit inside each service. In the proposal, the gateway checks one bucket per client in Redis before routing to any service . Each service limits on its own, so a client's real limit is the per-service limit × replicas × services. One bucket per client, checked once at the gateway: the limit holds however many services and replicas there are."},{"id":"request-path","heading":"The request path","text":"The gateway makes one Redis round trip per request. The script refills and takes tokens atomically, so concurrent requests can never spend the same token twice. Client Gateway Redis Orders GET /orders EVALSHA bucket refill + take, atomically allowed · 7.4 left GET /orders 200 OK a burst empties the bucket GET /orders EVALSHA bucket denied · retry 0.4 s 429 · Retry-After: 1 The service only ever sees admitted requests; Redis answers in well under a millisecond in-region. A request reaches the gateway, keyed by its API key. The gateway runs the bucket script in Redis. Refill and take happen in one atomic step. Admitted requests are routed on as before. Once a burst empties the bucket, the gateway answers 429 itself, with a Retry-After the client can trust."},{"id":"limiter-script","heading":"The limiter script","text":"-- KEYS[1] = bucket key (one per API key) -- ARGV = capacity, rate (tokens/s), now (ms, from Redis TIME), cost local b, r, now, cost = tonumber(ARGV[1]), tonumber(ARGV[2]), tonumber(ARGV[3]), tonumber(ARGV[4]) local s = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts') local tokens = tonumber(s[1]) or b local ts = tonumber(s[2]) or now tokens = math.min(b, tokens + math.max(0, now - ts) / 1000 * r) local ok = tokens >= cost if ok then tokens = tokens - cost end redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now) redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(b / r * 1000) + 1000) return { ok and 1 or 0, tostring(tokens) } Inputs. The gateway passes capacity and rate from the client's plan, plus the current time from Redis itself, so gateway clocks never matter. Read the bucket. A client seen for the first time starts with a full bucket. Refill, then take. This is the same arithmetic as the in-process version. Running it as a script makes the read-modify-write atomic. Write back and expire. An idle key expires once it would be full anyway, so memory tracks active clients only. Answer. Admitted or not, plus the tokens left, which the gateway turns into Retry-After ."},{"id":"alternatives","heading":"Alternatives considered","text":"The gateway option wins under the weights we agreed in the kickoff. Move the weights to test how sensitive the choice is: favouring time to ship above accuracy brings back per-service limits. Scores 1–5, higher is better. Weights 0–5 say how much each criterion matters. Criterion Weight Per service Gateway + Redis CDN rules Accuracy of the limit 5 1 5 3 Latency added 3 5 3 4 Operational cost 3 4 3 4 Sees API keys and plans 4 5 5 1 Time to ship 2 5 3 4"},{"id":"results","heading":"Canary results","text":"We ran the gateway limiter for the noisiest 5% of clients for a week. Everyone else's tail latency stopped tracking those clients' bursts. Day Per-service limits Edge limiter Mon 380 371 Tue 412 214 Wed 395 188 Thu 441 181 Fri 404 179 The canary started on Tuesday. p99 for the other 95% of clients fell by more than half and stayed flat through the noisy clients' bursts."},{"id":"risks","heading":"Risks & mitigations","text":"Risk Likelihood Impact Mitigation Redis unavailable Low High Fail open after 5 ms; services' coarse safety limits still apply Hot key for one huge client Medium Medium Shard that client's bucket by region; reconcile every second Clock skew between gateways Low Low None needed: the script reads time from Redis"},{"id":"rollout","heading":"Rollout plan","text":"Week 1 Canary: noisiest 5% of clients Done. The results are above. Week 2 All clients, shadow mode Decide but don't reject; compare against per-service decisions. Week 3 Enforce at the edge Exit criterion: under 0.1% disagreement in shadow mode. Week 5 Retire per-service limiters Keep a coarse safety limit at 10× the plan rate."},{"id":"decision","heading":"Decision","text":"We will enforce client rate limits at the gateway with the Redis bucket script, failing open, and retire the per-service limiters. Recorded as ADR-002 , which supersedes ADR-001."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html","title":"Glossary","kind":"reference","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Reference Glossary Shared definitions for terms used across these documents. Defined words are underlined wherever they appear. Hover over one, or tap it, to read its definition without leaving the page. ADR An architecture decision record: a short, permanent note of one significant decision, covering its context, the options considered and the consequences. ADRs are superseded by new records rather than edited. Back-reference In LZ77 compression, an instruction to copy L bytes starting D bytes back in the output already produced, instead of storing them again. DEFLATE allows distances up to 32,768 bytes. Step by step Backoff Waiting longer after each failed attempt before retrying, usually doubling the delay each time. Without jitter , clients that failed together retry together. Barrier A GPU instruction that makes every thread in a threadgroup wait until all of them arrive, and makes their earlier memory writes visible to each other. There is no safe barrier across threadgroups. Burst A short spike of requests above the average rate. In a token bucket, the largest burst allowed is the bucket's capacity b . Cache miss A lookup that finds nothing in the cache, so the request falls through to the slower source of truth and usually back-fills the cache. Canonical Huffman code A Huffman code rebuilt from code lengths alone: symbols are sorted by length, then by value, and each gets the previous code plus one, shifted left when the length grows. A DEFLATE block sends only the lengths. Step by step Compute kernel A function that runs on the GPU once per thread of a dispatch . Each thread reads its own index to decide which part of the work it does. CRC-32 A 32-bit checksum computed by polynomial division over the bits of the data. ZIP and gzip store one per file so a decoder can detect corruption. Checksums of adjacent pieces can be combined, which allows computing it in parallel. Cyclic redundancy check (Wikipedia) DEFLATE The compression format inside ZIP, gzip and PNG: LZ77 back-references plus Huffman coding , organised in blocks. Its bit stream has no index, so decoding is serial. Step by step · RFC 1951 Dispatch One launch of a compute kernel over a grid of threads, divided into threadgroups . Dispatches in one command buffer run in order, which is the safe way to order work between threadgroups. Divergence When lanes of one SIMD group take different sides of a branch. The hardware runs each side in turn with the other lanes switched off, so the branch costs the sum of both sides. End-of-block Symbol 256 of DEFLATE 's literal/length alphabet. It closes every Huffman-coded block, so it needs a code of its own even though it appears once. Step by step Extra bits In DEFLATE , the plain bits that follow a length or distance symbol to pick an exact value, because each symbol stands for a range: symbol 270 means a length from 23 to 26, and the 2 extra bits after it say which. They are written as a plain number, not Huffman-coded, and the symbol alone decides how many there are. Step by step GCRA The generic cell rate algorithm. It is equivalent to a token bucket, but stores one timestamp per key: the theoretical arrival time of the next conforming request. Generic cell rate algorithm (Wikipedia) Huffman code A variable-length binary code that gives frequent symbols short bit patterns and rare ones long patterns. No code is a prefix of another, so a decoder can read them back to back without separators. Step by step · Huffman coding (Wikipedia) Jitter Randomness added to retry or polling delays, so many clients that start together don't keep acting in lock-step and overload a recovering service. Lane One thread's slot within a SIMD group . Each lane has its own registers, but all lanes execute the same instruction at the same time. lcov A plain-text coverage format that lists, per source file, how many times each line ran. Most coverage tools can write it, which makes it the common currency between them and tools like patchcov. Lea"}]},{"href":"docs/how-deflate-compresses.html","title":"How DEFLATE compresses","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Compression How DEFLATE compresses The format inside gzip, zlib, PNG and ZIP is two simple ideas stacked on top of each other: first remove the repetition, then remove the skew. This page builds both up from a single 35-byte string, and ends by reading a real block bit by bit. Compression here is two separate savings: remove repetition, then remove skew. DEFLATE does the first with LZ77 and the second with Huffman coding. The only awkward part is joining the two. It matters because nearly everything uses it: gzip and zlib, PNG images, ZIP files, and Content-Encoding: gzip on the web. The common misconception is that DEFLATE says how to compress. It only says how to decompress: for the same 35 bytes, zlib writes 15 and the encoder on this page writes 13, and both are valid. A series in three parts How DEFLATE compresses , this page: the two savings, how they join, and a block read bit by bit. How a DEFLATE block sends its own code : codes fitted to the data, and how a block describes them cheaply. DEFLATE format reference : every range, field and bit-order rule, for reading a stream or writing a decoder."},{"id":"question","heading":"A question to start","text":"How do you make abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra smaller without losing a single letter? Stored as plain bytes it takes 35 bytes, or 280 bits. Two things about it are wasteful. The same 11 letters appear three times, and the letter a makes up almost half of the text while c and d appear once per word. A good compressor should exploit both. Guess before you read on: compressed as a gzip file by zlib at its best setting (level 9), how many bytes do these 35 take? 33 bytes. That looks like almost no saving, but 18 of those bytes are the gzip wrapper: a 10-byte header, then a CRC-32 and the original length (8 bytes) at the end. The DEFLATE stream inside is 15 bytes , 43% of the input. By the end of this page you will have read, bit by bit, a stream that does it in 13, and seen why the two differ."},{"id":"two-ideas","heading":"Two ideas, stacked","text":"DEFLATE runs two stages, and each removes a different kind of waste. LZ77 turns the bytes into a shorter list of tokens : a literal byte, or an instruction to copy earlier bytes. Huffman coding then writes each token with as few bits as its frequency allows. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Huffman code output bits Notice that the two savings are independent. The token count falls because of repetition (35 bytes, 10 tokens); the bits per token fall because of skew. These four colours keep the same meaning in every figure below. The two stages are independent, and each is useful alone. A file of random English words has little exact repetition but plenty of skew. A file of one byte repeated is all repetition. DEFLATE needs both because real data has both. So let's take them in that order: LZ77 on its own, then Huffman coding on its own, then the one part that isn't obvious, how DEFLATE joins them. Then we read a real block bit by bit, and come back to the opening question with every bit accounted for. If you already know LZ77 and Huffman coding, skip to the join : it is the part you are least likely to have seen. If you'd rather experiment as you go, the playground runs all of it on your own text."},{"id":"lz77","heading":"LZ77: say it once, then point back","text":"Read the input left to right. Keep everything already read in a sliding window (DEFLATE's holds the last 32,768 bytes). At each position, look for the longest run of upcoming bytes that already appears in the window. If it is at least 3 bytes long, emit a back-reference : copy length bytes, starting distance bytes back. Otherwise emit the byte itself as a literal. Why 3? A short copy costs about 12 to 25 bits once it is coded, as the bit walk shows (its 4-byte copy takes 13). Two literals cost 16, so a 2-byte copy would rarely pay; three cost 24. Watch the second copy : it starts 12 bytes back but is 23 bytes long, so it reads bytes that the copy itself is still writing. Start with nothing. The cursor is at the first byte and the window behind it is empty, so there is nothing to point back to. Seven literals. None of a b r a c a d starts a run of 3 bytes seen before, so each one drops out of the input as a literal token. The window grows behind the cursor. A match. The next four bytes, abra , already appear 7 bytes back. Any run of 3 or more is worth replacing, and this one is 4 long. Emit (4, 7). The four bytes collapse into one token: copy 4 bytes from 7 back . Four letters cost one token. The space is new , so it goes out as a literal. A long match that overlaps. Everything that is left, 23 bytes, repeats what started 12 bytes back. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the cursor, so the copy runs into its own output. Emit (23, 12). The decoder copies one byte at a time, so when it writes byte 24 it reads byte 12, which this same copy wrote 12 steps earlier. 35 bytes are now 10 tokens. Overlap is not a corner case. It is how LZ77 encodes runs: aaaaaaaa is the literal a followed by (7, 1) , \"copy 7 bytes from 1 back\"."},{"id":"huffman","heading":"Huffman: short codes for common symbols","text":"LZ77 has removed the repetition, but it leaves every literal at a full 8 bits: the common a costs as much as the rare d . Eight bits per letter is fair only if every letter is equally likely. In abracadabra the counts are a×5, b×2, r×2, c×1 and d×1, so a should get the shortest code. A first try: hand out the shortest codes in order of count. a gets 0 , b 1 , r 10 , c 11 , d 100 . That looks cheap, but it can't be read back. The codes go out back to back with nothing between them, so 10 could be r , or b then a . The trouble is that b 's code is the start of r 's. So a usable code needs one property: no code may be the start of another . Then a decoder can stop the moment the bits so far match a code. Drawing the codes as a tree gives that property for free. Each 0 goes left and each 1 goes right, and the letters sit only at the leaves. A code then ends at a leaf, and nothing passes through a leaf to make a longer code. What is left is to choose the tree. Huffman's algorithm finds the best one for known counts, and it is greedy: repeatedly join the two rarest things. Here the symbols are the letters themselves, to keep the tree small. DEFLATE runs the same algorithm on the output of LZ77 instead, as the join below shows: on counts of literals, lengths and distances, not of raw bytes. Notice that a sits one step from the root and gets a 1-bit code, while the four rare letters share the bottom row with 3-bit codes. No code is the start of another, because every letter is a leaf. Count each letter. At 8 bits a letter, the 11 letters of abracadabra cost 88 bits. Line them up, rarest first. Each letter is now a leaf, weighted by its count. Join the two rarest: c and d, 1 each, become a node of weight 2. Again. Three things now weigh 2: b, r and the new node. Join any two. Here b and r make 4. Again: 2 and 4 make 6. Only a (5) and this node are left. The last join makes the root, 11, which is every letter. The common a joined last, so it ends up nearest the root. Read the codes. Label every left branch 0 and every right branch 1. A letter's code is the path from the root to it: a is 0 , c 100 , d 101 , b 110 , r 111 . Count the bits. A code's length is the letter's depth in the tree: a has 1 bit, the others 3. Weighted by the counts, the total falls from 88 bits to 23. As a table. The tree has done its job. What remains is each letter with its code and its length, back in the table it started from: a 0 , 1 bit; b , r , c and d 3 bits each. Go deeper: at the second join, three nodes tied at weight 2. Join a different pair and you get a different tree. Does the total change? No: it is still 23 bits. Join c+d with b instead, and the code lengths become a 1, r 2, b 3, c 4 and d 4. The tree is different, but 5×1 + 2×2 + 2×3 + 1×4 + 1×4 = 23 as well. Huffman's algorithm gives an optimal code, not the optimal code. Remember this one: that ambiguity is what DEFLATE removes when a block sends its own code, in part 2 ."},{"id":"joining","heading":"How the two ideas join","text":"The two tools don't fit together yet. Huffman coding works on a known alphabet of symbols, like the five letters of abracadabra , and counts how often each one appears. LZ77's output is not like that: it mixes literal bytes with back-references, and a back-reference is a pair of numbers, a length up to 258 and a distance up to 32,768. Before Huffman coding can start, every token has to become symbols from a fixed alphabet. That translation is the join. The two stages meet once per block, always in this order: LZ77 turns the block's bytes into tokens: literals and (length, distance) copies. Map every token to symbols from two alphabets, described below. This is the join. Code the symbols: one Huffman code for each alphabet. Where those codes come from is a later section . Write the block: a short header, then each token's symbols as their codes, then the end-of-block code. So Huffman coding never codes LZ77's tokens as they are. A literal passes through as its own symbol, but a copy has to become symbols first. The join is therefore a choice of alphabet for everything LZ77 can produce. The obvious alphabet gives every value its own symbol: one per byte, one for the end of the block, one per length from 3 to 258 and one per distance from 1 to 32,768, 33,281 symbols in all. Huffman coding would then count exact values and give the common ones short codes. Let's see what that costs. Look at what LZ77 actually produces. In the 381-byte paragraph from the playground , this page's encoder finds 32 copies at 29 different distances, and 26 of those distances turn up exactly once. Lengths do repeat (length 3 appears 13 times, length 5 nine times), but exact distances barely do. That breaks the obvious alphabet, and you can work out how with nothing but a budget. Some blocks use a code fixed by the format, which can't know which symbols are common ( below ). A code of L bits uses up 2 − L of all bit patterns, since no other code may start with it, and the shares can't add up to more than 1. Give the 256 bytes 9 bits each and they use half of it. The other 33,025 symbols, the lengths and distances, share the other half, so each gets about 16 bits. A copy is a length and a distance, about 32 bits, so copying 4 bytes would save almost nothing over sending them as 4 literals at 9 bits each, 36. A code fitted to the block's own counts doesn't escape either. Its codes are fine: the paragraph's distances still get 4- or 5-bit codes. But a block with its own code has to tell the decoder what that code is, and with this alphabet the description needs room for every distance a copy might have used. How a block describes its code is the subject of part 2 . Here we only need its size, measured with the same method for both alphabets. For the paragraph, with this page's encoder, describing the obvious code takes 567 bits, against 358 for DEFLATE's, and the block comes out 6% larger. On 16 KB of English documentation, where copies reach thousands of bytes back at 1,877 different distances, the description takes 13,075 bits against 718, and the block comes out 14% larger. So the waste is in naming exact distances. What does repeat is roughly how far back a copy reaches. In that 16 KB there are about 3 copies per distance value within 100 bytes back, and about 0.1 per value at 4,000 to 6,000 bytes back, yet neighbouring distances are about equally likely. Whatever replaces the obvious alphabet has to put how far back in the Huffman code, where its skew can be used, and send exactly which distance as plain bits, where a code would find nothing to save. DEFLATE does exactly that, with two changes: A length or distance is a range, then extra bits. Distance 12 has no symbol of its own. It is one symbol meaning \"9 to 12 bytes back\", followed by 2 plain bits that pick 12. The ranges double in width as they reach further back, so the symbol says roughly how far and its extra bits say exactly where. The extra bits are written as they are, not Huffman-coded, bec"},{"id":"join-example","heading":"The join, token by token","text":"Here is the mapping applied to the ten tokens from the window figure , and then the codes that turn the symbols into bits. Notice that the middle row is all Huffman coding ever sees. A back-reference becomes two symbols from two alphabets (square: literal/length; rounded: distance), each followed by plain extra bits, and the end-of-block symbol costs bits too. Start from LZ77's output: eight literals and two back-references. A literal is its own symbol. The byte a becomes the symbol for a in the literal/length alphabet ; its number there is the byte's value, 97, in small print. All eight literals map this way. A back-reference becomes two symbols. Length 4 is short and common, so it has a symbol of its own in the same alphabet, number 258. A symbol's number is only its place in the alphabet: 258 means \"length 4\", not length 258. Distance 7 falls in the range \"7–8 back\" of the second, distance alphabet , so 1 extra bit follows to pick 7. (23, 12) becomes \"lengths 23–26\" with 2 extra bits, then \"9–12 back\" with 2 more. The block must end. Symbol 256, end-of-block, comes from the literal/length alphabet, so it sits among the literals and lengths and gets a code like them. Huffman codes turn symbols into bits. Each symbol is replaced by its code from its own alphabet. For now, take the codes as given: this block uses a set of codes fixed by the format, 8 bits per literal, 7 for the end and the short lengths, 5 per distance, and the next section says where they come from, along with the 3 bits at the start that tell the decoder which codes to use. Extra bits go out as they are, uncoded. That makes 103 bits, the number in the first figure."},{"id":"block-types","heading":"Blocks, and the fixed code","text":"The join took the fixed codes for granted, so here is where they come from. A decoder can read the bits only if it knows which codes the encoder used, and there is more than one sensible choice. So DEFLATE splits a stream into blocks, and each block says at its start how it is coded. A stream is a series of blocks. Each block starts with 3 header bits: a last-block bit, 1 if this is the last block, then 2 bits for the block type, which say where the codes come from. The format calls them BFINAL and BTYPE , and the bit walks use those names. Two bits allow four types, and only three are used: The values of BTYPE . Header fields are read lowest bit first, so the bits arrive in the opposite order to how the value is written; the bit walk shows why that matters. BTYPE Arrives as Block 00 0 , then 0 stored: raw bytes, no compression 01 1 , then 0 fixed Huffman codes, set by the RFC 10 0 , then 1 dynamic Huffman codes, sent at the start of the block 11 1 , then 1 reserved: invalid, and a decoder must reject the stream (zlib reports \"invalid block type\") The rest of this page uses the fixed codes, type 01: a table of codes set once by the RFC, so a block sends no table at all. Every literal from 0 to 143, which covers all of ASCII, gets 8 bits, and 144–255 get 9. End-of-block and the length symbols get 7 or 8, and every distance symbol gets 5. The join figure above used these codes, and the next section reads that block bit by bit. Dynamic codes, which a block fits to its own data, are the subject of part 2 . Go deeper: why are the fixed codes 7–9 bits long? It is the budget from the join again: a code of L bits uses up 2 − L of all possible bit patterns, and the shares can't add up to more than 1. The fixed code covers 288 symbols, two more than the 286 of the literal/length alphabet: 286 and 287 are never sent, but they fill out the code. Without knowing which symbols are common, spread evenly that is about log 2 288 ≈ 8.2 bits each. The RFC spends the budget exactly: Symbols Count Bits Share of the budget bytes 0–143 (all of ASCII) 144 8 144 / 256 = 0.5625 bytes 144–255 112 9 112 / 512 = 0.21875 256–279: end of block and short lengths 24 7 24 / 128 = 0.1875 280–287: long lengths (286 and 287 unused) 8 8 8 / 256 = 0.03125 total 288 1 The 7-bit codes for end-of-block and common lengths are paid for by making the upper half of the byte values, rare in text, cost 9 bits. Only a code fitted to the data can make a common symbol short, by making rare ones longer: that is what a dynamic block's code does."},{"id":"one-block","heading":"One real block, bit by bit","text":"Let's read the stream this page's encoder writes for the opening string, the 13 bytes 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 01 51 0a 38 d8 00 , the way a decoder reads them. These are the bits after Huffman coding: each literal, length and distance appears as its code from the fixed code, not as a raw byte or number. It holds exactly the tokens of the window figure , so every bit below is one you have already seen produced in the join . One convention first, because it trips everyone up. A decoder takes the bits of each byte lowest first. So the block type 01, fixed, arrives as 1 then 0 , and a decoder that read those two bits the other way round would see 10 and try to read a dynamic block. Plain numbers, like the block type and the extra bits, come out right read lowest bit first, because that is how they were packed. Huffman codes are the exception: they are packed most significant bit first, because the decoder walks down the code from the root one bit at a time and needs the first step of the path first. The format reference has the whole rule. Here is the first byte, 4b , done by hand. In binary it is 01001011 . Read from its lowest bit, the right-hand end, it gives 1 1 0 1 0 0 1 0 : BFINAL = 1, then the block type's two bits, 1 then 0 , which is 01, then 10010 , the first five bits of a 's code. The next byte, 4c , supplies the last three, 001 . A code may run across a byte boundary; only the order of the bits matters. The walk below shows every bit already in reading order. 1 BFINAL = 1: last block 10 BTYPE = 01: fixed codes 10010001 'a', a literal (symbol 97) 10010010 'b', a literal (symbol 98) 10100010 'r', a literal (symbol 114) 10010001 'a', a literal (symbol 97) 10010011 'c', a literal (symbol 99) 10010001 'a', a literal (symbol 97) 10010100 'd', a literal (symbol 100) 0000010 length 4 (symbol 258) 00101 7–8 back (d5) 0 + 1 extra bit = 0: distance 7 01010000 ' ', a literal (symbol 32) 0001110 lengths 23–26 (symbol 270) 00 + 2 extra bits = 0: length 23 00110 9–12 back (d6) 11 + 2 extra bits = 3: distance 12 0000000 end of block (symbol 256) The block header. The last-block bit, BFINAL , is 1, so this is the last block. The two block-type bits, BTYPE , arrive as 1 0 , lowest first, so the type is binary 01: the fixed codes (see the block types ). For 35 bytes, a code fitted to the text would cost more to describe than it saves. Seven literals, 8 bits each. In the fixed code, bytes 0–143 are 00110000 plus the byte, so a (97) is 10010001 . (The fixed codes are handed out in order, shortest first. The 24 seven-bit codes take 0000000 to 0010111 , so the 8-bit ones start at 00110000 ; part 2 explains the rule.) That is no shorter than the raw byte, 01100001 : the fixed code isn't fitted to this text, so it gives every byte from 0 to 143 the same 8 bits ( why ). This block's whole saving comes from LZ77's two copies. A back-reference, (4, 7). Length 4 has a symbol of its own, 258, with no extra bits. Symbols 256–279 have the 7-bit codes, so each one's code is simply the symbol minus 256: 258 is 0000010 . The distance falls in the range 7–8 back, and its one extra bit, 0, picks 7. 13 bits stand for 4 bytes. Another literal: the space (32). The long, overlapping copy, (23, 12). The symbol for lengths 23–26, then 2 extra bits, 00 , that add 0: length 23. Then 9–12 back, plus 11 , 3: distance 12. 16 bits stand for 23 bytes. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the destination, but the copy is 23 long. Watch the output , counting bytes from 0: the decoder copies one byte at a time, so when it reaches byte 24 it reads byte 12, which this same copy wrote 12 steps earlier. Every byte it reads already exists by the time it needs it. One thing this block can't show you: its extra bits, 0 , 00 and 11 , read the same either way round, so you can't see that they arrive lowest bit first. Part 2 has one that doesn't. End of block. Symbol 256 is seven zeros in the fixed code. That makes 103 bits, the total in the first figure; one more bit of padding"},{"id":"fixed-limits","heading":"Where the fixed code falls short","text":"The fixed code is fair to every byte, and that is its weakness: it can't know which bytes are common. For the 35-byte opening string that didn't matter, and the fixed block was the right choice. But take a longer text, a 381-byte paragraph of English about DEFLATE (one of the playground's examples, further down ). zlib compresses it with codes fitted to its own counts, built with the same algorithm as the abracadabra tree : Some literals in zlib's block for the paragraph: 233 literals, using 38 different bytes. Byte Times Fixed code Fitted code space 32 8 bits 3 bits e 20 8 bits 4 bits i 16 8 bits 4 bits s , t 15 each 8 bits 4 bits o 14 8 bits 4 bits k , u , v , x 1 each 8 bits 8 bits The common bytes drop to 3 or 4 bits, and the rare ones lose nothing. Across all 233 literals the fixed code spends 1,864 bits and the fitted code 1,108, about 4.8 bits a literal instead of 8. With the lengths and distances counted too, the data takes 2,375 bits with the fixed code and 1,521 with the fitted one. The fitted code isn't free: the decoder has to be told what it is, and for this block that costs 352 bits. Even so, with the 3 header bits the block comes to 1,876 bits instead of 2,378, about a fifth smaller. That is the case for dynamic codes, and part 2 is about them: how a block describes its code in a few hundred bits rather than thousands, by sending nothing but code lengths."},{"id":"answer","heading":"Back to the question","text":"Before the tally: the block is 177 bits smaller than the 280 plain bits. How many of those 177 come from removing repetition, and how many from removing skew? A rough split is enough. All of them come from repetition, and a little more. The two copies replace 216 plain bits with 29, a saving of 187. The literals save nothing: the fixed code gives every ASCII byte 8 bits, exactly what it cost before, so the skew of abracadabra is never used. The header and end-of-block cost 10 bits that plain bytes don't, and 187 − 10 = 177. If you gave skew a fair share, that was the reasonable model: Huffman coding does remove skew, but only with a code fitted to the text, and for 35 bytes describing one costs more than it saves. So how do you make abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra smaller without losing a letter? You remove its two kinds of waste, in order, and you can now account for every one of the 103 bits: Where the 13 bytes go, against the 280 bits of the plain text. Part of the block Bits Plain bits it replaces header: last-block bit and block type 3 0 7 literals, abracad 56 56 (4, 7), a copy of abra 13 32 the space, a literal 8 8 (23, 12), a copy of 23 bytes 16 184 end of block 7 0 total 103 280 All of the saving is repetition: two copies stand for 27 of the 35 bytes, in 29 bits. Skew doesn't help at all here: the fixed code gives every ASCII byte the same 8 bits it had before. Codes fitted to the string would save 59 bits on the data but cost 159 bits to describe ( part 2 writes that block out), which is why the block uses the fixed codes. And zlib's 15 bytes are the same story with a slightly worse parse: 16 bits worse . The same two questions explain any compression result you meet: is there repetition, and is there skew? English text has both, which is why the paragraph compresses well. A JPEG, or anything already compressed or encrypted, has neither left, which is why gzipping it gains nothing. PNG is the clever case: before DEFLATE runs, it filters each row of pixels, usually storing each byte as its difference from a neighbouring pixel. A smooth image then becomes mostly small numbers, and that is skew Huffman coding can use. How good is DEFLATE, then? RFC 1951, which defines it, dates from 1996. Newer formats such as Zstandard and Brotli use the same two ideas with longer windows and more sophisticated coding, and usually compress better. DEFLATE stays everywhere because every decoder already reads it."},{"id":"try-it","heading":"Try it","text":"You have seen both savings on one 35-byte string. Here they are on any text you like. Type anything, or pick an example. The tokens are what LZ77 found. The numbers show each saving on its own and both together: a code fitted to the bytes removes only skew, LZ77 with the fixed code removes only repetition, and the last does both. A fitted code's count includes the bits to describe it. Every count is the size of a real DEFLATE stream that this page's encoder writes, not an estimate. Text to compress 35 bytes abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra ×3 A paragraph One letter ×300 No repeats 280 bits as plain bytes 223 bits with a fitted code alone, no LZ77 103 bits with LZ77 and the fixed code 203 bits with LZ77 and a fitted code, 159 of them to describe it 13 bytes out, as a fixed block Write it as the smallest stored fixed dynamic 13 bytes ( 103 bits) as a fixed block: 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 01 51 0a 38 d8 00 LZ77 replaced 27 of 35 bytes with 2 back-references, leaving 8 literals. Codes built for this text would take 159 bits to describe, more than they save, so the fixed codes win. For comparison, your browser's own DEFLATE ( CompressionStream ) wrote … bytes. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Click a back-reference to see the bytes it copies. a b r a c a d (4,7) ␣ (23,12) The token stream as a table # Token Stands for At byte 1 a a 0 2 b b 1 3 r r 2 4 a a 3 5 c c 4 6 a a 5 7 d d 6 8 (4,7) abra 7 9 ␣ ␣ 11 10 (23,12) abracadabra abracadabra 12 Click any back-reference to see which earlier bytes it copies, and use Write it as to see the same tokens as each kind of block: the smallest is what an encoder would send, but every one decodes. The paragraph shows both savings clearly: LZ77 finds the repeated words, and the skew of English letters pays for describing a code fitted to it. One letter ×300 is almost all repetition. \"No repeats\" has nothing for LZ77 and too little text to pay for describing a code, so it comes out slightly larger than it went in. A question to try: pick the paragraph, then delete text from its end. Below what length does the smallest block switch from dynamic to fixed? Guess first. Optional: the answer From 69 bytes on, this page's encoder writes a dynamic block; at 68 and below, a fixed one. Describing a fitted code costs about 230 bits even for a short text, so it pays only once there are enough symbols to save that much. zlib, which chooses its tokens differently, switches at 74 ( part 2 )."},{"id":"serial","heading":"Why decoding is serial","text":"Everything so far has been about size. The price of that density is speed. The output has no separators and no index. A code's length is known only once it has been decoded, so the decoder cannot find where symbol n + 1 starts without decoding symbol n . Back-references add a second chain: a copy reads bytes that earlier tokens wrote. That is why a gzip file is decompressed by one core, front to back, however many cores the machine has. Getting around it, for example to decode on a GPU, means finding where blocks start without decoding what comes before, and resolving copies whose sources aren't decoded yet. A later series here takes that on; nothing on this page depends on it. Next in the series: How a DEFLATE block sends its own code , where a block fits a Huffman code to its own text and has to tell the decoder what that code is."},{"id":"terms","heading":"Terms used on this page","text":"These are defined here, for this page. DEFLATE's broader terms are in the site glossary : DEFLATE , LZ77 , Huffman code , canonical Huffman code , back-reference , sliding window , end-of-block and extra bits . Alphabet The fixed set of symbols a Huffman code is built over. DEFLATE uses three: literal/length and distance for the data, and the code-length alphabet, which part 2 uses to send a block's own code. How the two ideas join BFINAL The first bit of every block: 1 if it is the last block of the stream. Blocks, and the fixed code BTYPE The 2 bits after BFINAL that give the block type: 00 stored, 01 fixed Huffman codes, 10 dynamic Huffman codes. 11 is invalid. Like every plain number, they arrive lowest bit first. The values Distance alphabet The 30 symbols, d0–d29, that stand for back-reference distances from 1 to 32,768. Most stand for a range, picked out by extra bits. It has a Huffman code of its own. How the two ideas join Dynamic Huffman code Codes built for one block from that block's own symbol counts, and sent as code lengths at its start ( BTYPE 10). They don't change while the block is read: dynamic means per block, not adaptive. A dynamic block, bit by bit Fixed Huffman code Codes set once by the RFC, for any block with BTYPE 01, so the block never has to describe them: 8 or 9 bits per literal, 7 or 8 per length symbol, 5 per distance symbol. Why 7–9 bits Literal A byte that LZ77 sends as itself, because no match of 3 or more starts there. It becomes the literal/length symbol with the same value, 0–255. LZ77 Literal/length alphabet The 286 symbols 0–285: 0–255 for literal bytes, 256 for the end of the block, and 257–285 for ranges of lengths. One Huffman code covers them all, so a single code says whether a literal or a copy comes next. How the two ideas join Overlapping copy A back-reference whose length is greater than its distance, so it reads bytes it is itself writing. It works because the decoder copies one byte at a time; distance 1 simply repeats a byte. The bit walk shows one Prefix code A code in which no codeword is the start of another, so codes can be read back to back without separators. Every Huffman code is a prefix code. Huffman Stored block A block of up to 65,535 raw bytes ( BTYPE 00), after a byte-aligned length and its complement. Encoders use it when compressing would make the data bigger. Choosing a block type Symbol One element of an alphabet: what Huffman coding counts and gives a code. DEFLATE maps LZ77's tokens onto symbols: a literal is its own symbol, a back-reference becomes two plus extra bits. How the two ideas join Token One item of LZ77's output: a literal, or a back-reference (length, distance). Each becomes one or two symbols. LZ77"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"RFC 1951, DEFLATE : the format itself, short and readable. Section 3.2 has the alphabets and the canonical code. RFC 1952, gzip and RFC 1950, zlib : the two wrappers, a few header bytes and a checksum around the same stream. Huffman (1952), \"A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes\" : the original paper. Ziv and Lempel (1977), \"A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression\" : where LZ77 gets its name. zlib's algorithm.txt : how zlib's encoder chooses matches, which this page leaves out."}]},{"href":"docs/how-deflate-sends-its-code.html","title":"How a DEFLATE block sends its own code","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Compression How a DEFLATE block sends its own code A Huffman code fitted to the text saves bits only if the decoder can be told what it is, and telling it costs bits too. This page tries a table, a tree and a list of code lengths, and shows why DEFLATE sends only the lengths, even though for a tiny block the tree would be cheaper. To ship a code along with its data, send the least that determines it, in an order the receiver can predict. A DEFLATE block sends only each symbol's code length, in symbol order, and the decoder rebuilds exactly the same codes by a rule both sides know. It matters because the description is pure overhead: for a 381-byte paragraph, zlib's fitted code saves 854 bits on the data and describing it costs 352. The common misconception is that the Huffman tree travels with the data. It doesn't: no tree and no symbol names are sent. A series in three parts How DEFLATE compresses : the two savings, how they join, and a block read bit by bit. How a DEFLATE block sends its own code , this page: codes fitted to the data, and how a block describes them cheaply. DEFLATE format reference : every range, field and bit-order rule, for reading a stream or writing a decoder."},{"id":"recap","heading":"Where part 1 left off","text":"Part 1 built DEFLATE's two savings and read a block written with the fixed codes, which the format sets once, so a block never has to describe them. The one idea to bring from it: a code fitted to a block's own counts saves bits on the data, but the decoder has never seen those counts, so the block has to tell it the code. For a 381-byte paragraph of English, zlib's fitted code saved 854 bits on the data, and describing it cost 352 of them. Describe it carelessly and the code costs more than it saves. This page is about that description. First we fit a code to part 1's 35-byte example, abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra , so there is something concrete to send. Then we look for the cheapest way to send it: as a table, as a tree, and then the idea that makes DEFLATE's dynamic blocks affordable, sending nothing but code lengths. The rest of the page squeezes that list until, for the paragraph, it beats the tree, and then reads a whole dynamic block bit by bit."},{"id":"example-code","heading":"The example's own code","text":"Fitting a code pays for the paragraph. To follow one all the way through, here is the code fitted to the 35-byte example. Huffman coding never sees the raw letters: it counts the symbols that LZ77's output maps to ( the join ). After LZ77 the example is 10 tokens. Its 8 literals, its two length symbols and the end-of-block symbol make 11 symbols in the literal/length alphabet, 9 of them different: a appears 3 times, as a literal, and every other symbol once. The two distances belong to the distance alphabet, which gets a code of its own below. Notice that this is not the letters' tree from earlier. LZ77 has already replaced most of the a s with copies, so a is 3 of 11 symbols here, not 5 of 11, and gets 2 bits instead of 1. Count the symbols. The 8 literals, the two length symbols 258 and 270, and end-of-block. With the fixed code these 11 symbols cost 85 bits. Join the rarest, in pairs. Eight symbols tie at 1. Joining two at a time, lowest symbol number first, makes four nodes of 2: ␣ with b , c with d , r with end, 258 with 270. Again. The four nodes of 2 pair up into two nodes of 4. Now a is one of the two rarest. With 3 it is lighter than either 4, so it joins one of them to make 7. The last join makes the root, 11: every symbol. Read the codes. Label each left branch 0 and each right branch 1. A symbol's code is the path from the root to it: a is 10 , r is 000 , ␣ is 1100 . No code is the start of another, because every symbol is a leaf. Count the bits. A code's length is the symbol's depth in the tree: a has 2 bits; r , end, 258 and 270 have 3; ␣, b , c and d have 4. The 11 symbols now cost 34 bits instead of 85. As a table. The tree has done its job. What remains is each symbol with its code and its length, in the order of the tree's leaves. The next section starts from exactly this table. The distance code is simpler: d5 and d6 are used once each, so each gets 1 bit, against 5 in the fixed code. Add the 5 extra bits, which no code touches, and the example's data costs 34 + 2 + 5 = 41 bits, against 85 + 10 + 5 = 100 with the fixed code: 59 bits saved. But the decoder has never seen the example's counts, so it can't build the tree itself: the block has to tell it the code, and every bit spent on that comes out of those 59."},{"id":"canonical","heading":"Send lengths, not trees","text":"How should a block tell the decoder its code? The most literal way is a table. For each used symbol, write its number in 9 bits, enough for all 286 literal/length symbols, then its code's length in 4 bits so the decoder knows where the code ends, then the code itself. For the example that is 9 × (9 + 4) bits plus the codes themselves, 30 bits in all, 147 bits, and d5 and d6 add 2 × (5 + 4 + 1) = 20: 167 bits, plus something to say where the table ends. That is four times the 41 bits of data it describes, and nearly three times the 59 bits the code saves. Sending the tree is cheaper, because the codes are implied by its shape and need not be written out. Walk it from the root, left branch before right. At a branch write 0; at a leaf write 1, then the leaf's symbol number. The decoder rebuilds the same tree from those bits: a 0 means two subtrees follow, a 1 means a symbol follows. Against the example's tree , the walk starts 0 0 0 1 r : the root, the node of 4 on its left, the node of 2 on that one's left, then the leaf r , followed by its sibling, 1 end. Notice how little of the tree is tree. The 0s and 1s that give its shape are the thin slivers; the symbol names are most of the bar. A name is the symbol's number, 0–285, in 9 bits: a is symbol 97, 001100001 . It is neither a 's code nor the byte it decodes to. Each block is drawn as wide as the bits it costs. That is 111 bits for the example. Look at where they go: 20 describe the shape , and 91 name the symbols . Neither is what the decoder actually needs. The names. The tree needs them because its leaves come in tree order, so each leaf must say which symbol it is. A list in symbol order needs none, because the decoder counts as it reads: the first length is symbol 0's, the second symbol 1's, so the 98th is symbol 97's, which is a 's. Nothing in the stream says 97; the position does. Below is the whole trip, from that slot to the byte a . The shape. As the tie-break note in part 1 showed (when nodes tie, joining a different pair gives a different tree with the same total), many trees are equally good. Swap the two halves under the node of 7: a becomes 11 , and ␣, b , c and d become 1000 to 1011 . Every one of those codes changes, but no length does, so the data still costs 34 bits. The tree spends bits picking one code out of many that are all as good. So DEFLATE drops both, and this is the step the rest of the page builds on. It fixes one rule for turning code lengths into codes, called the canonical Huffman code , so no shape is sent. And it sends the lengths as a list in symbol order, so no symbol is named. Notice that the codes come from the order alone. Once the symbols are sorted by length and then by symbol number, each code is the previous one plus one, and gains a 0 on the right for each bit it grows longer. Start from the tree's codes, in the order of its leaves: r 000 , end 001 , 258 010 , 270 011 , a 10 , then ␣, b , c and d 1100 to 1111 . Throw the codes away. Keep only each symbol's length. This is all a DEFLATE block sends. Sort by length, and by symbol number within a length: a (97); then r (114), end (256), 258 and 270; then ␣ (32), b , c and d . The first code is all zeros, as long as the shortest length: a gets 00 . Count up, then shift. Add one to the last code ( 01 ), then append a 0 for the extra bit of length: r gets 010 . Within a length, count up: end 011 , 258 100 , 270 101 . Again for 4 bits. 101 + 1 is 110 , and one more bit makes ␣ 1100 ; then b 1101 , c 1110 , d 1111 . The decoder runs the same rules on the same lengths, so it gets the same codes without ever seeing a tree. These are not the tree's codes: a was 10 and is now 00 , and r was 000 and is now 010 . But every symbol keeps its length, so the 11 symbols still cost 34 bits. Counting up keeps the code prefix-free: only a 's code starts 00 , and only the 4-bit codes start 11 . The distance code works the same way: d5 and d6, 1 bit each, get 0 and 1 . These are the codes the example's dyna"},{"id":"dynamic-header","heading":"Sending a dynamic code","text":"A dynamic block must tell the decoder its codes before its data. Thanks to canonical codes , it only needs to send each symbol's code length: 286 literal/length symbols and 30 distance symbols, 316 lengths in all, each from 0 to 15. The question is how to send them cheaply, and it matters: every bit spent on the code comes out of the saving. For the paragraph, the fitted code saved 854 bits on the data, and sending it cost 352 of them. Here are four tries, each fixing the biggest waste in the one before, measured on two blocks: the worked example written as a dynamic block (the one walked through below ), and zlib's real block for the 381-byte paragraph. Before the four tries: the tree costs 111 bits for the example and 581 for the paragraph. Will DEFLATE's final list of lengths beat the tree on the example, on the paragraph, on both or on neither? Pick one. On the paragraph only. DEFLATE's list costs 352 bits for the paragraph, against the tree's 581, but 159 for the example, against 111. The tree pays for each symbol a block uses, about 11 bits each; the list pays a little for every symbol it could use. The example uses 11 symbols and the paragraph 57, so only the paragraph has enough names for dropping them to pay. \"Both\" is the natural guess, since the page has argued for lengths all along, and \"neither\" is reasonable once you see how many slots the list has. The answer depends on the block. Real blocks, with thousands of symbols, are on the paragraph's side."},{"id":"step-naive","heading":"1. Every length, 4 bits each","text":"Write all 316 lengths as plain 4-bit numbers, in symbol order. That is 1,264 bits, 158 bytes, in every dynamic block whatever it holds. The example's data is only 41 bits, so describing its code would cost 30 times as much as the data itself. Even the paragraph's data, 1,521 bits, would nearly double. It is also more than ten times the tree's 111 bits for the example: the tree pays about 11 bits for each symbol a block uses, but the list pays 4 bits for every symbol it could use. Steps 2 to 4 cut what the unused ones cost."},{"id":"step-counts","heading":"2. Say how many","text":"Any one block uses few of the symbols. So send two counts first, how many literal/length lengths follow and how many distance lengths (plain 5-bit numbers the format calls HLIT and HDIST , each stored as the count minus a fixed offset), and stop each list after its highest used symbol; the rest count as 0. Guess before you read on: the example uses only 11 of its 316 symbols. How much should cutting each list after its last used symbol save? Hardly anything. It barely helps: 1,122 bits for the example and 1,158 for the paragraph, against 1,264. Those are the lengths that remain, at 4 bits each, plus 10 bits for the two counts. The unused symbols are mostly in the middle of the alphabet, the byte values a text never contains, not at the end, so there is little tail to cut. The counts stay in the final scheme anyway: with no separators, they are how the decoder knows where each list ends. To shrink the list, step 3 has to deal with the zeros in the middle."},{"id":"dynamic-runs","heading":"Runs, and a code for the runs","text":""},{"id":"step-runs","heading":"3. Replace runs with one symbol","text":"Write the lengths with a small alphabet of 19 symbols instead: 0–15 for a length, 16 to repeat the previous length, and 17 and 18 for runs of zeros, each followed by plain extra bits that give the count. For now, write each of these symbols as a plain 5-bit number. A whole stretch of unused byte values becomes one symbol: the example's 262 unused literal/length symbols take just 7. The header drops to 146 bits for the example ( step 3 in the figure below ) and 443 for the paragraph. That is the big win, but the paragraph's header is still 443 bits. The code-length alphabet. Fixed by the format: the same in every dynamic block. Each range follows from its extra bits: 2 bits give 4 counts, 3 give 8, 7 give 128. Symbol Means Extra bits Count 0–15 a code length of 0 to 15 0 16 repeat the previous length 2 3–6 times 17 a run of zeros 3 3–10 18 a run of zeros 7 11–138 Runs start at 3 because a run of 1 or 2 is cheaper written out. Zeros get two run symbols, a short one and a long one, because they are by far the most common length. A run's extra bits are a plain number, so they arrive lowest bit first, and they count up from the shortest run the symbol allows. The example's first run is the 32 unused byte values 0–31: an 18, then the 7 extra bits 1010100 . Read backwards that is 0010101 , 21, and an 18's runs start at 11, so 11 + 21 = 32."},{"id":"step-coded","heading":"4. Huffman-code the code-length symbols","text":"Step 3 still spends 5 bits on every one of these code-length symbols, yet they are far from equally common. The paragraph's lengths take 78 of them: symbols 8 and 5 appear 14 times each and 0 appears 12 times, while 2 appears once and 16 not at all. That is the same skew Huffman coding removes from the data, one level down, so DEFLATE gives these symbols a Huffman code too: the code-length code , a Huffman code whose job is to send the other two codes. Its own 19 lengths go first, as plain 3-bit numbers. A dynamic block is therefore a stack of three layers, each written with the one below it: The three layers of a dynamic block, from the top. A decoder reads them from the bottom up. Layer Written with Size the data the literal/length and distance codes the whole block those two codes' lengths the code-length code up to 316 lengths the code-length code's lengths plain 3-bit numbers at most 19 × 3 = 57 bits It stops at three layers because the bottom one is so small: 57 bits at most, too little for yet another code to repay its own description. This is DEFLATE's actual scheme, and it costs 159 bits for the example ( step 4 in the figure below ) and 352 for the paragraph. For the paragraph, coding the code-length symbols saved another 91 bits, 443 down to 352. For the tiny example it cost 13 more: describing the small code took more than it saved on just 19 symbols. DEFLATE has no way to stop at step 3, which is one more reason tiny blocks are written with the fixed codes. And against the tree? For the example, the tree still wins, 111 bits to 159: a block with only 11 different symbols has too few names to be worth dropping. For the paragraph, which uses 46 literal/length symbols and 11 distances, the tree would cost 581 bits, 469 of them names, against 352 for the lengths. Real blocks run to thousands of symbols and use far more of the alphabet, so the gap only grows. Here are the four steps side by side, on the example's own list of 316 lengths, each drawn to scale against its 41 bits of data. Step through them and watch where the bits go. Notice what the four steps do to the same list: the long bar is almost all zeros, cutting its end barely shortens it, and only collapsing the zeros into run symbols shrinks it. The bar is the cost to scale, against a mark at the example's 41 bits of data: even at its best, the code costs more than the data it describes. The strip at the bottom shows what each step's cost is made of, as shares of that step, with the arithmetic under each part. Each box in the middle row is one code-length symbol; under it is what it stands for (×32 means a run of 32 zeros), its code once it has one, a run's extra bits as they arrive, and its cost in bits. Step 1: every slot, 4 bits. All 316 of the example's code lengths, in symbol order. Only the 11 marked slots are not zero. 1,264 bits. Step 2: say how many. HLIT and HDIST cut each list after its last used symbol, 270 and d6, so 38 slots go: 278 lengths at 4 bits, plus 10 bits for the two counts, make 1,122 bits. The zeros in the middle are still there. Step 3: runs. Each stretch of zeros collapses into one run symbol: 18 for 11–138 zeros, 17 for 3–10. Each used length becomes one symbol too. The grey wedges show which stretch of the old list each symbol replaced. Read each column down. Symbol sent is what goes in the stream; stands for is what it means (×32 is a run of 32 zeros, a is a 's length); extra bits give a run's count, read backwards ; bits is what it costs: 5 for the symbol, plus its extra bits. The 19 symbols take 95 + 41 = 136 bits; with the 10 bits of counts, 146. Step 4: code the code-length symbols. 18, 3 and 4 get 2-bit codes, 17 gets 3 bits, 1 gets 4, and 0 and 2 get 5. The symbols and their extra bits now take 50 + 41 = 91 bits. With 14 bits of counts and the 54 bits that describe this small code, that makes 159 bits: for this short list, 13 more than step 3. Step The example The paragraph The tree, to beat 111 581 1. 4 bits each 1264 1264"},{"id":"dynamic-block","heading":"The same tokens as a dynamic block","text":"Part 1 read the example as a fixed block, 103 bits. To see every part of a dynamic Huffman block, here are the same tokens written as one by the same encoder: it is the \"dynamic\" choice under Write it as . Dynamic means the codes are built for this one block, from its own counts, and sent at its start. They don't change while the block is read. The result first, so you can skip the walk: the dynamic block is 203 bits, against the fixed block's 103. Describing the codes takes 159 bits, and the codes save only 59 on the data ( the table at the end has every part). The walk below reads all 26 bytes, 75 c6 31 15 00 00 08 42 c1 2a 56 fb 6a 02 fa 0f 3c 02 30 dd b1 e2 f8 30 e5 06 , bit by bit. It is there for when you want to read or write a dynamic block yourself; nothing later on this page depends on it. This is step 4 in full. It has the codes for the data, and the code-length code that sends their lengths. Keep the levels apart, because the walk below sends them bottom-up, the code-length code first: The three alphabets, and where the walk below defines and uses each one's code. The alphabets are the same in every dynamic block; the symbols used and their codes are this block's. Level Alphabet Symbols used in this block Their codes in this block Code defined in lines Code used in lines the data literal/length 9: a , b , r , c , d , ␣, 256, 258, 270 2, 3 or 4 bits 24–39 43–55 the data distance 2: d5, d6 1 bit each 40–42 51, 54 the lengths code-length (CL) 7: 1, 2, 3, 4 (the lengths above), 0, 17, 18 (for zeros) 2 to 5 bits 6–23 24–42 1 BFINAL = 1: last block 01 BTYPE: 10 = 2, dynamic 01110 HLIT: 01110 = 14, + 257 = 271 01100 HDIST: 00110 = 6, + 1 = 7 0111 HCLEN: 1110 = 14, + 4 = 18 000 CL symbol 16: 000 = 0, unused 110 CL symbol 17: 011 = 3 bits 010 CL symbol 18: 010 = 2 bits 101 CL symbol 0: 101 = 5 bits 000 CL symbol 8: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 7: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 9: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 6: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 10: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 5: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 11: 000 = 0, unused 010 CL symbol 4: 010 = 2 bits 000 CL symbol 12: 000 = 0, unused 010 CL symbol 3: 010 = 2 bits 000 CL symbol 13: 000 = 0, unused 101 CL symbol 2: 101 = 5 bits 000 CL symbol 14: 000 = 0, unused 001 CL symbol 1: 100 = 4 bits 10 +1010100 18: 32 zeros, 0–31 01 4: ␣ (32) gets 4 bits 10 +1010110 18: 64 zeros, 33–96 11111 2: a (97) gets 2 bits 01 4: b (98) gets 4 bits 01 4: c (99) gets 4 bits 01 4: d (100) gets 4 bits 10 +0100000 18: 13 zeros, 101–113 00 3: r (114) gets 3 bits 10 +1111111 18: 138 zeros, 115–252 110 +000 17: 3 zeros, 253–255 00 3: 256 (end) gets 3 bits 11110 0: 257 unused 00 3: 258 gets 3 bits 10 +0000000 18: 11 zeros, 259–269 00 3: 270 gets 3 bits 110 +010 17: 5 zeros, d0–d4 1110 1: d5 gets 1 bit 1110 1: d6 gets 1 bit 00 literal a (97) 1101 literal b (98) 010 literal r (114) 00 literal a (97) 1110 literal c (99) 00 literal a (97) 1111 literal d (100) 100 length symbol 258 → 4 0 +0 distance d5 → 7 1100 literal ␣ (32) 101 +00 length symbol 270 → 23 1 +11 distance d6 → 12 011 end of block (256) 00000 padding (not read) The block header. BFINAL is 1. BTYPE arrives as 0 , then 1 : binary 10, a dynamic block. Every code from here on is defined by the block itself. How many lengths follow. The code lengths come next as a bare list in symbol order: the first is symbol 0's, the second symbol 1's, with no symbol numbers and nothing to mark where the list ends and the data begins. So the block says first how long each list is. HLIT : 271 literal/length lengths, for symbols 0–270. 270 is the highest symbol this block uses, so the encoder stops there and leaves out 271–285. HDIST : 7 distance lengths, d0–d6. HCLEN : 18 of the 19 code-length-code lengths. The names are the RFC's: they count the lit eral/length, dist ance and c ode- len gth codes. The RFC never says what the H stands for, though it is usually read as Huffman. All three are plain numbers, which arrive lowest bit first, so each line shows the"},{"id":"decoding","heading":"Decoding with only the lengths","text":"The round trip said what happens to one symbol: its slot, its code, its byte. This section is how a decoder does the middle step quickly, and nothing after it depends on it; if you are happy to trust that it can, skip to choosing a block type . The one thing to take from it: the decoder turns the lengths into a lookup table, then decodes each symbol with a single lookup. The middle step, from bits to a code, is where the work is. The codes are 2, 3 and 4 bits long, and nothing in the data marks where one ends, so the decoder can't simply cut the data into codes. It could read one bit at a time and check, after each, whether the bits so far make a code: up to 4 checks per symbol here, and up to 15 in a block with 15-bit codes. Instead it builds a table that answers both questions at once: given the next few bits, which symbol do they start with, and how many bits is its code? Here it is for the example, from start to finish: from the lengths the block sent, to its codes, to its table, and then the table at work on the example's own data. Notice that only the lengths on the left come from the block; the decoder makes everything else from them. A short code fills several table entries and a long one fills one, and the window always peeks at 4 bits but moves only as far as the entry says. The data bits are the example block's real ones, as the decoder reads them. Start from what the block sent: one length per symbol, in symbol order, 271 of them. The decoder knows each one's symbol by counting: the 98th is symbol 97, a , and it is 2. 262 of the 271 are 0, unused; they are folded into ranges here. Only 9 symbols have a code. Turn the lengths into codes, by the canonical rule : sort the 9 used symbols by length, then by symbol number, and count up. a , the only length 2, gets 00 ; the four 3s get 010 to 101 ; the four 4s get 1100 to 1111 . The encoder did the same, so these are its codes. Fill a 's entries. The longest code is 4 bits, so the table has one entry for every 4-bit pattern: 2 4 = 16. a 's code, 00 , is only 2 bits. Any 4 bits that start 00 begin with a , whatever the other two are, so a fills all four entries from 0000 to 0011 . Each one says: a , 2 bits. The longer codes fill fewer entries. Each 3-bit code fills two: r 0100 and 0101 , end 0110 and 0111 , 258 1000 and 1001 , 270 1010 and 1011 . Each 4-bit code fills one, ␣ to d , 1100 to 1111 . That makes 4 + 4 × 2 + 4 × 1 = 16: every entry is filled exactly once, so any 4 bits the decoder meets begin with exactly one code. Decode: peek at 4 bits. The data starts 0011 . Entry 0011 says a , 2 bits. The decoder outputs a and consumes only those 2 bits: the other two are the start of the next code. The window moves on by 2. The next 4 bits are 1101 : b , 4 bits. Output b , consume 4. 0100 : r , 3 bits. The decoder always peeks at 4 bits, and the entry says how far to move. It never needs to know in advance where a code ends. Four more lookups: 0011 a , 1110 c , 0011 a , 1111 d . Output so far: abracad . 1000 : 258, 3 bits. The last of those 4 bits isn't 258's: it was peeked at, not consumed. 258 is not a byte but a copy length, 4, so a distance comes next, looked up in the distance table, built the same way from the block's two distance lengths: 0 is d5, and its extra bit, 0 , makes the distance 7. The decoder copies abra from 7 bytes back: abracadabra . So a decoder builds its lookup tables itself, at the start of every dynamic block, from the lengths the block sends. It builds one for literals and lengths and one for distances, and, as the bit walk showed, one more before those, to read the lengths themselves. Only then can it read the data. A new dynamic block brings new lengths, so the tables are built again. A fixed block's lengths never change, so its tables are built once and reused. Go deeper: what zlib's tables look like A decoder could follow the code one bit at a time, but zlib looks up several bits at once. Its literal/length table is indexed by the next 9 bits "},{"id":"choosing","heading":"Choosing a block type","text":"An encoder now has a choice for every block, and it matters. The example's dynamic block is twice the size of its fixed one, while the paragraph's dynamic block is a fifth smaller than its fixed one. And for data with no repetition or skew, such as random bytes, both codes make things worse: the fixed code spends 8 or 9 bits on every byte. That is what the third type, the stored block, is for: it copies the bytes as they are. Here are the three side by side. Stored (00) No compression: up to 65,535 raw bytes after a 4-byte length and its complement. Caps the cost of incompressible data at about 5 bytes per 64 KiB: the 3 header bits, padded to a byte, then the 4-byte length No saving at all Fixed Huffman (01) Codes set by the RFC: 8 or 9 bits for a literal, 7 or 8 for a length, 5 for a distance. No table to send Wins on short inputs Ignores this data's skew Dynamic Huffman (10) Codes built for this block's own counts, sent as code lengths at the start of the block. Removes the skew too Usually wins once a block holds more than a few dozen bytes of text Describing the code costs tens of bytes Any input can be written as any of the three, and every one is valid: a decoder accepts whichever it gets. Which one wins is a sum, not a rule. The encoder works out the exact size of each kind for every block (zlib ends a block after about 16,000 symbols) and writes the smallest. Tiny blocks come out fixed because describing a code costs more than it saves: cut the paragraph in the chart down to its first few dozen bytes, and zlib 1.2.12 switches from fixed to dynamic once the text is 74 bytes long. Input Stored Fixed Dynamic zlib -9 abracadabra ×3 (35 B) 114.3 37.1 74.3 42.9 A paragraph (381 B) 101.3 78.2 62.2 61.7 Random bytes (1 KiB) 100.5 105.6 105.2 100.5 Each input has a different winner. The short string can't pay for a table; the paragraph can; random bytes can't be compressed at all, so storing them is cheapest. The first three columns are the same tokens written as each block type; zlib picks per block."},{"id":"what-it-costs","heading":"What a block's code costs","text":"Back to the paragraph from the start . Its fitted code saves 854 bits on the data, and the description that makes that possible costs 352: three counts and the code-length code's own lengths, then the 78 code-length symbols that spell out the lengths. Sent as a tree, the same code would cost 581 bits; as a plain list of 316 lengths, 1,264, more than the whole saving. Each step on this page is what turned a code that doesn't pay into one that does. The pattern is worth keeping, because it turns up wherever a format has to ship a model along with its data: send the least that determines the model (lengths, not codes), in an order the receiver can predict (position, not names), and compress the description with the same tool, one level down , until it is too small to repay another level. DEFLATE's own limit is per block. Every dynamic block sends a complete new code, even when the previous block's would do. For 16 KB of English text, describing the code takes 718 bits, about 1.3% of the block, so this matters little; for small blocks it is most of the block, which is why they stay fixed. Later formats can reuse a table across blocks, as Zstandard does . Next in the series: the DEFLATE format reference , with every range, field and bit-order rule in one place."},{"id":"terms","heading":"Terms used on this page","text":"These are defined here, for this page. DEFLATE's broader terms are in the site glossary : DEFLATE , LZ77 , Huffman code , canonical Huffman code , back-reference , sliding window , end-of-block and extra bits . Alphabet The fixed set of symbols a Huffman code is built over. DEFLATE uses three: literal/length and distance for the data, and the code-length alphabet, which part 2 uses to send a block's own code. How the two ideas join Code length The number of bits in a symbol's Huffman code. 0 means the symbol has no code. A dynamic block sends only the lengths; the codes follow from them by the canonical rule. Send lengths, not trees Code-length code The small Huffman code a dynamic block uses to write down its code lengths. Its alphabet has 19 symbols: 0–15 are lengths, 16 repeats the previous length, and 17 and 18 are runs of zeros. CL is short for code length. Huffman-code the code-length symbols Dynamic Huffman code Codes built for one block from that block's own symbol counts, and sent as code lengths at its start ( BTYPE 10). They don't change while the block is read: dynamic means per block, not adaptive. A dynamic block, bit by bit Fixed Huffman code Codes set once by the RFC, for any block with BTYPE 01, so the block never has to describe them: 8 or 9 bits per literal, 7 or 8 per length symbol, 5 per distance symbol. Why 7–9 bits HCLEN 4 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many code-length-code lengths follow, minus 4. Those lengths come in a fixed order, so the count can cut off the usually-unused ones at the end. Why that order HDIST 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many distance code lengths follow, minus 1. In the bit walk HLIT 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many literal/length code lengths follow, minus 257. The lengths come as a bare list in symbol order, so the count says where the list ends. In the bit walk Literal/length alphabet The 286 symbols 0–285: 0–255 for literal bytes, 256 for the end of the block, and 257–285 for ranges of lengths. One Huffman code covers them all, so a single code says whether a literal or a copy comes next. How the two ideas join Prefix code A code in which no codeword is the start of another, so codes can be read back to back without separators. Every Huffman code is a prefix code. Huffman Stored block A block of up to 65,535 raw bytes ( BTYPE 00), after a byte-aligned length and its complement. Encoders use it when compressing would make the data bigger. Choosing a block type Symbol One element of an alphabet: what Huffman coding counts and gives a code. DEFLATE maps LZ77's tokens onto symbols: a literal is its own symbol, a back-reference becomes two plus extra bits. How the two ideas join"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"RFC 1951, DEFLATE : the format itself, short and readable. Section 3.2 has the alphabets and the canonical code. RFC 1952, gzip and RFC 1950, zlib : the two wrappers, a few header bytes and a checksum around the same stream. Huffman (1952), \"A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes\" : the original paper. Ziv and Lempel (1977), \"A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression\" : where LZ77 gets its name. zlib's algorithm.txt : how zlib's encoder chooses matches, which this page leaves out."}]},{"href":"docs/how-deflate-works.html","title":"How DEFLATE works","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Compression How DEFLATE works The format inside gzip, zlib, PNG and ZIP is two simple ideas stacked on top of each other. First remove the repetition, then remove the skew. This page builds each one up, step by step, from a single 35-byte string. DEFLATE makes two separate savings. LZ77 replaces each repeat with a short (length, distance) pointer back to an earlier copy. Huffman coding then gives common symbols short bit patterns and rare ones long patterns. It matters because nearly everything uses it: gzip and zlib, PNG images, ZIP files, and Content-Encoding: gzip on the web. The common misconception is that the Huffman tree travels with the data. It doesn't. Only the code lengths are sent, and the codes are rebuilt from them by counting."},{"id":"question","heading":"A question to start","text":"How do you make abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra smaller without losing a single letter? Stored as plain bytes it takes 35 bytes, or 280 bits. Two things about it are wasteful. The same 11 letters appear three times, and the letter a makes up almost half of the text while c and d appear once per word. A good compressor should exploit both. Guess before you read on: compressed as a gzip file by zlib at its best setting (level 9), how many bytes do these 35 take? 33 bytes. That looks like almost no saving, but 18 of those bytes are the gzip wrapper: a 10-byte header, then a CRC-32 and the original length (8 bytes) at the end. The DEFLATE stream inside is 15 bytes , 43% of the input. By the end of this page you will have taken it apart bit by bit, and seen an encoder that does it in 13."},{"id":"two-ideas","heading":"Two ideas, stacked","text":"DEFLATE runs two stages, and each removes a different kind of waste. LZ77 turns the bytes into a shorter list of tokens : a literal byte, or an instruction to copy earlier bytes. Huffman coding then writes each token with as few bits as its frequency allows. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Huffman code output bits Notice that the two savings are independent. The token count falls because of repetition (35 bytes, 10 tokens); the bits per token fall because of skew. These four colours keep the same meaning in every figure below. The two stages are independent, and each is useful alone. A file of random English words has little exact repetition but plenty of skew. A file of one byte repeated is all repetition. DEFLATE needs both because real data has both."},{"id":"lz77","heading":"LZ77: say it once, then point back","text":"Read the input left to right. Keep everything already read in a sliding window (DEFLATE's holds the last 32,768 bytes). At each position, look for the longest run of upcoming bytes that already appears in the window. If it is at least 3 bytes long, emit a back-reference : copy length bytes, starting distance bytes back. Otherwise emit the byte itself as a literal. Watch the second copy : it starts 12 bytes back but is 23 bytes long, so it reads bytes that the copy itself is still writing. Start with nothing. The cursor is at the first byte and the window behind it is empty, so there is nothing to point back to. Seven literals. None of a b r a c a d starts a run of 3 bytes seen before, so each one drops out of the input as a literal token. The window grows behind the cursor. A match. The next four bytes, abra , already appear 7 bytes back. Any run of 3 or more is worth replacing, and this one is 4 long. Emit (4, 7). The four bytes collapse into one token: copy 4 bytes from 7 back . Four letters cost one token. The space is new , so it goes out as a literal. A long match that overlaps. Everything that is left, 23 bytes, repeats what started 12 bytes back. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the cursor, so the copy runs into its own output. Emit (23, 12). The decoder copies one byte at a time, so by the time it needs byte 13 of the copy, it has already written it. 35 bytes are now 10 tokens. Overlap is not a corner case. It is how LZ77 encodes runs: aaaaaaaa is the literal a followed by (7, 1) , \"copy 7 bytes from 1 back\"."},{"id":"huffman","heading":"Huffman: short codes for common symbols","text":"LZ77 has removed the repetition, but it leaves every literal at a full 8 bits: the common a costs as much as the rare d . Eight bits per letter is fair only if every letter is equally likely. In abracadabra the counts are a×5, b×2, r×2, c×1 and d×1, so a should get the shortest code. Huffman's algorithm finds the best codes for known counts, and it is greedy: repeatedly join the two rarest things. Here the symbols are the letters themselves, to keep the tree small. DEFLATE runs the same algorithm on the output of LZ77 instead, as the join below shows: on counts of literals, lengths and distances, not of raw bytes. Notice that a sits one step from the root and gets a 1-bit code, while the four rare letters share the bottom row with 3-bit codes. No code is the start of another, because every letter is a leaf. Count each letter. At 8 bits a letter, the 11 letters of abracadabra cost 88 bits. Line them up, rarest first. Each letter is now a leaf, weighted by its count. Join the two rarest: c and d, 1 each, become a node of weight 2. Again. Three things now weigh 2: b, r and the new node. Join any two. Here b and r make 4. Again: 2 and 4 make 6. Only a (5) and this node are left. The last join makes the root, 11, which is every letter. The common a joined last, so it ends up nearest the root. Read the codes. Label every left branch 0 and every right branch 1. A letter's code is the path from the root to it: a is 0 , c 100 , d 101 , b 110 , r 111 . Count the bits. A code's length is the letter's depth in the tree: a has 1 bit, the others 3. Weighted by the counts, the total falls from 88 bits to 23. As a table. The tree has done its job. What remains is each letter with its code and its length, back in the table it started from. At the second join, three nodes tied at weight 2. Join a different pair and you get a different tree. Does the total change? No: it is still 23 bits. Join c+d with b instead, and the code lengths become a 1, r 2, b 3, c 4 and d 4. The tree is different, but 5×1 + 2×2 + 2×3 + 1×4 + 1×4 = 23 as well. Huffman's algorithm gives an optimal code, not the optimal code. That ambiguity is what DEFLATE removes when a block sends its own code, further on ."},{"id":"joining","heading":"How the two ideas join","text":"The two tools don't fit together yet. Huffman coding works on a known alphabet of symbols, like the five letters of abracadabra , and counts how often each one appears. LZ77's output is not like that: it mixes literal bytes with back-references, and a back-reference is a pair of numbers, a length up to 258 and a distance up to 32,768. Before Huffman coding can start, every token has to become symbols from a fixed alphabet. That translation is the join. The two stages meet once per block, always in this order: LZ77 turns the block's bytes into tokens: literals and (length, distance) copies. Map every token to symbols from two alphabets, described below. This is the join. Count the symbols and build two Huffman codes from the counts: one for literals and lengths, one for distances. Write the block: its header (for a dynamic block, including the two codes), then each token's symbols as Huffman codes, each followed by its extra bits, then the end-of-block code. So Huffman coding never sees the raw bytes, or the tokens themselves. It counts and codes the symbols LZ77's output maps to, while the extra bits travel alongside, uncoded. The join is therefore a choice of alphabet for everything LZ77 can produce. The obvious alphabet would give every possible value its own symbol: b0 – b255 for the bytes, eob , w3 – w258 for the lengths and d1 – d32768 for the distances, 33,281 symbols in one code. DEFLATE does not do that. Its alphabets are drawn below, all 316 symbols. Pick something LZ77 can produce and see which symbol it becomes. LZ77 gives a byte end of block a length a distance Value 23 Not DEFLATE: one symbol per value w23 one of 33,281 symbols DEFLATE 270 + extra bits 0 Symbol 270 of the literal/length alphabet stands for lengths 23&#8288;–&#8288;26. 2 extra bits after it give the offset: 23 + 0 = 23. Literal/length alphabet : 286 symbols, one Huffman code bytes 0–255 256 257 258 259 260 261 262 263 264 265 266 267 268 269 270 271 272 273 274 275 276 277 278 279 280 281 282 283 284 285 Distance alphabet : 30 symbols, its own Huffman code d0 d1 d2 d3 d4 d5 d6 d7 d8 d9 d10 d11 d12 d13 d14 d15 d16 d17 d18 d19 d20 d21 d22 d23 d24 d25 d26 d27 d28 d29 Symbol 270 covers 4 lengths. The extra bits say which one: 23 +0 24 +1 25 +2 26 +3 The two changes, one at a time: A length or distance is a range symbol plus extra bits. Length 23 is not a symbol of its own. It is symbol 270, which means \"a length from 23 to 26\", followed by 2 plain extra bits, 00 , that pick 23. The extra bits are written as they are, not Huffman-coded. Short lengths (3–10) and distances (1–4) still get exact symbols, because they are the most common. Distances are coded separately. The bytes, the end of block and the length ranges share the first alphabet, so one code tells the decoder whether a literal or a copy comes next. A distance always follows a length, so the decoder always knows to read it with the second code."},{"id":"join-example","heading":"The join, token by token","text":"Here is the mapping applied to the ten tokens from the window figure , and then the codes that turn the symbols into bits. Notice that the middle row is all Huffman coding ever sees. A back-reference becomes two symbols from two alphabets (square: literal/length; rounded: distance), each followed by plain extra bits, and the end-of-block symbol costs bits too. Start from LZ77's output: eight literals and two back-references. A literal is its own symbol. The byte a (97) is symbol 97 of the literal/length alphabet , which runs from 0 to 285. All eight literals map this way. A back-reference becomes two symbols. Length 4 is symbol 258 of the same alphabet. Distance 7 is code d5 of a second, distance alphabet ; d5 covers distances 7–8, so 1 extra bit follows to pick 7. (23, 12) becomes 270, covering lengths 23–26, with 2 extra bits, then d6, covering 9–12, with 2 more. The block must end. Symbol 256, end-of-block, comes from the literal/length alphabet, so it sits among the literals and lengths and gets a code like them. Huffman codes turn symbols into bits. Each symbol is replaced by its code from its own alphabet's table. This block uses the fixed tables: 8 bits per literal, 7 for symbols 256–279, 5 per distance code. Extra bits go out as they are, uncoded. With the 3 header bits that is 103 bits, the number in the first figure."},{"id":"two-alphabets","heading":"Every range, and the decoder's loop","text":"The full mapping, for when you need the exact ranges: How LZ77's output maps onto DEFLATE's two alphabets. Where a symbol covers a range, plain extra bits follow it to pick the exact value; they are not Huffman-coded. LZ77 gives Symbol Alphabet Extra bits In the example a literal byte, 0–255 0–255, the byte's own value literal/length 0 a (byte 97) → 97 the end of the block 256 literal/length 0 256 , once at the end a length, 3–10 257–264, one each literal/length 0 length 4 → 258 a length, 11–257 265–284, each covering 2–32 lengths literal/length 1–5 length 23 → 270 ( 23–26 ) + 2 bits a length of 258 285 literal/length 0 a distance, 1–4 d0–d3, one each distance 0 a distance, 5–32,768 d4–d29, each covering 2–8,192 distances distance 1–13 distance 7 → d5 ( 7–8 ) + 1 bit; 12 → d6 ( 9–12 ) + 2 bits The decoder's inner loop runs the join backwards. Its two tables are the two alphabets: while True: sym = read_code(lit_len_table) # literal, length or end if sym < 256: out.append(sym) # a literal byte elif sym == 256: break # end of block else: i = sym - 257 length = LENGTH_BASE[i] + read_bits(LENGTH_EXTRA[i]) d = read_code(distance_table) # always follows a length distance = DIST_BASE[d] + read_bits(DIST_EXTRA[d]) for _ in range(length): # byte by byte: may overlap out.append(out[-distance])"},{"id":"block-types","heading":"Blocks, and the fixed code","text":"The join figure turned symbols into bits with \"the fixed tables\", taking them for granted. But a decoder can read the bits only if it knows which codes the encoder used, and there is more than one sensible choice. So DEFLATE splits a stream into blocks, and each block says at its start how it is coded. A stream is a series of blocks. Each block starts with 3 header bits: BFINAL (1 if this is the last block) and the 2-bit BTYPE , which says where the codes come from. Two bits allow four values, and only three are used: The values of BTYPE . Header fields are read lowest bit first, so the bits arrive in the opposite order to how the value is written. BTYPE Arrives as Block 00 0 , then 0 stored: raw bytes, no compression 01 1 , then 0 fixed Huffman codes, set by the RFC 10 0 , then 1 dynamic Huffman codes, sent at the start of the block 11 1 , then 1 reserved: invalid, and a decoder must reject the stream (zlib reports \"invalid block type\") The rest of this half of the page uses the fixed codes ( BTYPE 01): a table of codes set once by the RFC, so a block sends no table at all. Every literal from 0 to 143, which covers all of ASCII, gets 8 bits, and 144–255 get 9. End-of-block and the length symbols get 7 or 8, and every distance symbol gets 5. The join figure above used these codes, and so did zlib for the opening string; the next section reads its block bit by bit. Dynamic codes, which a block fits to its own data, come after that. Go deeper: why are the fixed codes 7–9 bits long? A prefix code has a budget. A code of L bits uses up 2 − L of all possible bit patterns, since nothing else may start with it, and the shares can't add up to more than 1. The fixed code must cover 288 symbols without knowing which are common, and spread evenly that is about log 2 288 ≈ 8.2 bits each. The RFC spends the budget exactly: Symbols Count Bits Share of the budget bytes 0–143 (all of ASCII) 144 8 144 / 256 = 0.5625 bytes 144–255 112 9 112 / 512 = 0.21875 256–279: end of block and short lengths 24 7 24 / 128 = 0.1875 280–287: long lengths (286 and 287 unused) 8 8 8 / 256 = 0.03125 total 288 1 The 7-bit codes for end-of-block and common lengths are paid for by making the upper half of the byte values, rare in text, cost 9 bits. Only a code fitted to the data can make a common symbol short, by making rare ones longer: that is what a dynamic block's code does."},{"id":"one-block","heading":"One real block, bit by bit","text":"These are the bits after Huffman coding: each literal, length and distance appears as its code from the fixed table, not as a raw byte or number. Here is what the reference zlib (version 1.2.12) actually wrote at level 9 for the opening string: the 15 bytes 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 49 04 52 0a 89 d8 d9 00 , read the way a decoder reads them. Bits are taken from each byte lowest first. Huffman codes are read one bit at a time, most significant first; header fields and extra bits are plain numbers, lowest bit first. 1 BFINAL = 1: last block 10 BTYPE = 01: fixed codes 10010001 literal 97 'a' (byte 01100001) 10010010 literal 98 'b' 10100010 literal 114 'r' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 10010011 literal 99 'c' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 10010100 literal 100 'd' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 0000001 length symbol 257: length 3 00101 distance code 5: distances 7–8 0 + 1 extra bit = 0: distance 7 01010000 literal 32 ' ' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 0001101 length symbol 269: lengths 19–22 11 + 2 extra bits = 3: length 22 00110 distance code 6: distances 9–12 11 + 2 extra bits = 3: distance 12 0000000 end of block (symbol 256) The block header. BFINAL is 1, so this is the last block. The next two bits arrive as 1 0 , lowest first, so BTYPE is binary 01: the fixed Huffman codes (see the BTYPE values ). For 35 bytes, a dynamic table would cost more than it saves. Eight literals, 8 bits each. In the fixed code, bytes 0–143 are 00110000 plus the byte, so a (97) is 10010001 . That is no shorter than the raw byte, 01100001 : the fixed code isn't fitted to this text, so it gives every byte from 0 to 143 the same 8 bits ( why ). This block's whole saving comes from LZ77's two copies. Notice also that zlib wrote the second a of abra as a literal. A back-reference, (3, 7). Length symbol 257 means 3, with no extra bits. Distance code 5 covers 7–8, and its one extra bit, 0, picks 7. zlib copied bra where the window figure copied abra : 13 bits for 3 bytes. Two more literals: the space (32) and another a . The long, overlapping copy, (22, 12). Symbol 269 covers lengths 19–22, and the extra bits 11 add 3: length 22. Distance code 6 covers 9–12, plus 3: distance 12. 16 bits stand for 22 bytes. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the destination, but the copy is 22 long. Watch the output : the decoder copies one byte at a time, so when it reaches byte 25 it reads byte 13, which this same copy wrote 12 steps earlier. Every byte it reads already exists by the time it needs it. End of block. Symbol 256 is seven zeros in the fixed code. That makes 119 bits; one more bit of padding fills the 15th byte. Replay the copy The decoder's output for zlib's stream. A copy always reads to the left of where it writes. Striped bytes are read by the copy that wrote them: the copy starts 12 bytes back but runs for 22, so from byte 25 on it reads its own output. Why is zlib's version bigger than the figure's? zlib wrote 119 bits; the parse in the window figure needs 103. DEFLATE fixes how to decode , not how to encode: any token stream that rebuilds the input is valid. zlib's hash chains use position 0 as their \"no match\" marker, so zlib never copies from the very first byte of a stream. Both of its copies here would have started there, so each starts one byte later, after an extra literal a : 16 bits lost. On real files, zlib's smarter match choices win by far more. Other builds choose differently again: Chromium's fork of zlib, in Chrome and in Node's official binaries, writes 17 bytes for this string."},{"id":"fixed-limits","heading":"Where the fixed code falls short","text":"The fixed code is fair to every byte, and that is its weakness: it can't know which bytes are common. For the 35-byte opening string that didn't matter, and zlib was right to use it. But take a longer text, a 381-byte paragraph of English about DEFLATE (one of the playground's examples, further down ). zlib compresses it with codes fitted to its own counts, built with the same algorithm as the abracadabra tree : Some literals in zlib's block for the paragraph: 233 literals, using 38 different bytes. Byte Times Fixed code Fitted code space 32 8 bits 3 bits e 20 8 bits 4 bits i 16 8 bits 4 bits s , t 15 each 8 bits 4 bits o 14 8 bits 4 bits k , u , v , x 1 each 8 bits 8 bits The common bytes drop to 3 or 4 bits, and the rare ones lose nothing. Across all 233 literals the fixed code spends 1,864 bits and the fitted code 1,108, about 4.8 bits a literal instead of 8. With the lengths and distances counted too, the data takes 2,375 bits with the fixed code and 1,521 with the fitted one. The fitted code isn't free: the decoder has to be told what it is, and for this block that costs 352 bits. Even so, the block comes to 1,876 bits instead of 2,378, about a fifth smaller. That is the case for dynamic codes. To see one built from start to finish, the next section fits a code to the example; the sections after it work out how a block sends its code cheaply."},{"id":"example-code","heading":"The example's own code","text":"Fitting a code pays for the paragraph. To follow one all the way through, here is the code fitted to the 35-byte example. Huffman coding never sees the raw letters: it counts the symbols that LZ77's output maps to ( the join ). After LZ77 the example is 10 tokens plus the end-of-block symbol: 11 symbols, 9 of them different. a appears 3 times, as a literal; every other symbol appears once. Notice that this is not the letters' tree from earlier. LZ77 has already replaced most of the a s with copies, so a is 3 of 11 symbols here, not 5 of 11, and gets 2 bits instead of 1. Count the symbols. The 8 literals, the two length symbols 258 and 270, and end-of-block. With the fixed code these 11 symbols cost 85 bits. Join the rarest, in pairs. Eight symbols tie at 1. Joining two at a time, lowest symbol number first, makes four nodes of 2: ␣ with b , c with d , r with end, 258 with 270. Again. The four nodes of 2 pair up into two nodes of 4. Now a is one of the two rarest. With 3 it is lighter than either 4, so it joins one of them to make 7. The last join makes the root, 11: every symbol. Read the codes. Label each left branch 0 and each right branch 1. A symbol's code is the path from the root to it: a is 10 , r is 000 , ␣ is 1100 . No code is the start of another, because every symbol is a leaf. Count the bits. A code's length is the symbol's depth in the tree: a has 2 bits; r , end, 258 and 270 have 3; ␣, b , c and d have 4. The 11 symbols now cost 34 bits instead of 85. As a table. The tree has done its job. What remains is each symbol with its code and its length, in the order of the tree's leaves. The next section starts from exactly this table. The distance code is simpler: d5 and d6 are used once each, so each gets 1 bit. This code takes the example's symbols from 85 bits to 34. But the decoder has never seen the example's counts, so it can't build the tree itself: the block has to tell it the code, and every bit spent on that comes out of the 51 bits saved."},{"id":"canonical","heading":"Send lengths, not trees","text":"How should a block tell the decoder its code? The most literal way is a table. For each used symbol, write its number in 9 bits, enough for all 286 literal/length symbols, then its code's length in 4 bits so the decoder knows where the code ends, then the code itself. For the example that is 9 × (9 + 4) bits plus the 30 bits of the codes, 147 bits, and d5 and d6 add 2 × (5 + 4 + 1) = 20: 167 bits, plus something to say where the table ends. That is four times the 41 bits of data it describes, and more than three times the 51 bits the code saves. Sending the tree is cheaper, because the codes are implied by its shape and need not be written out. Walk it from the root, left branch before right. At a branch write 0; at a leaf write 1, then the leaf's symbol number. The decoder rebuilds the same tree from those bits: a 0 means two subtrees follow, a 1 means a symbol follows. Against the example's tree , the walk starts 0 0 0 1 r : the root, the node of 4 on its left, the node of 2 on that one's left, then the leaf r , followed by its sibling, 1 end. Notice how little of the tree is tree. The 0s and 1s that give its shape are the thin slivers; the symbol names are most of the bar. A name is the symbol's number, 0–285, in 9 bits: a is symbol 97, 001100001 . It is neither a 's code nor the byte it decodes to. Each block is drawn as wide as the bits it costs. That is 111 bits for the example. Look at where they go: 20 describe the shape , and 91 name the symbols . Neither is what the decoder actually needs. The names. The tree needs them because its leaves come in tree order, so each leaf must say which symbol it is. A list in symbol order needs none, because the decoder counts as it reads: the first length is symbol 0's, the second symbol 1's, so the 98th is symbol 97's, which is a 's. Nothing in the stream says 97; the position does. Below is the whole trip, from that slot to the byte a . The shape. As the tie-break question showed, many trees are equally good. Swap the two halves under the node of 7: a becomes 11 , and ␣, b , c and d become 1000 to 1011 . Every one of those codes changes, but no length does, so the data still costs 34 bits. The tree spends bits picking one code out of many that are all as good. So DEFLATE drops both. It fixes one rule for turning code lengths into codes, called the canonical Huffman code , so no shape is sent. And it sends the lengths as a list in symbol order, so no symbol is named. Notice that the codes come from the order alone. Once the symbols are sorted by length and then by symbol number, each code is the previous one plus one, and gains a 0 on the right for each bit it grows longer. Start from the tree's codes, in the order of its leaves: r 000 , end 001 , 258 010 , 270 011 , a 10 , then ␣, b , c and d 1100 to 1111 . Throw the codes away. Keep only each symbol's length. This is all a DEFLATE block sends. Sort by length, and by symbol number within a length: a (97); then r (114), end (256), 258 and 270; then ␣ (32), b , c and d . The first code is all zeros, as long as the shortest length: a gets 00 . Count up, then shift. Add one to the last code ( 01 ), then append a 0 for the extra bit of length: r gets 010 . Within a length, count up: end 011 , 258 100 , 270 101 . Again for 4 bits. 101 + 1 is 110 , and one more bit makes ␣ 1100 ; then b 1101 , c 1110 , d 1111 . The decoder runs the same rules on the same lengths, so it gets the same codes without ever seeing a tree. These are not the tree's codes: a was 10 and is now 00 , and r was 000 and is now 010 . But every symbol keeps its length, so the 11 symbols still cost 34 bits. Counting up keeps the code prefix-free: only a 's code starts 00 , and only the 4-bit codes start 11 . The distance code works the same way: d5 and d6, 1 bit each, get 0 and 1 . These are the codes the example's dynamic block uses, in the bit walk further on."},{"id":"decoding","heading":"Decoding with only the lengths","text":"So the decoder gets from a bare list of lengths to the right bytes without any symbol ever being named. For a : Its slot. The decoder counts the lengths as it reads them. Slot 97, the 98th length, is a 's because a is symbol 97, and it holds 2. Its code. Once it has every length, the decoder runs the canonical rule above and builds a lookup table from code to symbol. a is the only symbol of length 2, so it gets 00 , as it did in the encoder. Its byte. In the data, the decoder reads 00 , looks it up and gets symbol 97. A symbol below 256 is a literal, so it outputs byte 97, 01100001 : a . Step 2 is where the work is. The codes are 2, 3 and 4 bits long, and nothing in the data marks where one ends, so the decoder can't simply cut the data into codes. It could read one bit at a time and check, after each, whether the bits so far make a code: up to 4 checks per symbol here, and up to 15 in a block with 15-bit codes. Instead it builds a table that answers both questions at once: given the next few bits, which symbol do they start with, and how many bits is its code? Here it is for the example, from start to finish: from the lengths the block sent, to its codes, to its table, and then the table at work on the example's own data. Notice that only the lengths on the left come from the block; the decoder makes everything else from them. A short code fills several table entries and a long one fills one, and that the window always peeks at 4 bits but moves only as far as the entry says. The data bits are the example block's real ones, as the decoder reads them. Start from what the block sent: one length per symbol, in symbol order, 271 of them. The decoder knows each one's symbol by counting: the 98th is symbol 97, a , and it is 2. 262 of the 271 are 0, unused; they are folded into ranges here. Only 9 symbols have a code. Turn the lengths into codes, by the canonical rule : sort the 9 used symbols by length, then by symbol number, and count up. a , the only length 2, gets 00 ; the four 3s get 010 to 101 ; the four 4s get 1100 to 1111 . The encoder did the same, so these are its codes. Fill a 's entries. The longest code is 4 bits, so the table has one entry for every 4-bit pattern: 2 4 = 16. a 's code, 00 , is only 2 bits. Any 4 bits that start 00 begin with a , whatever the other two are, so a fills all four entries from 0000 to 0011 . Each one says: a , 2 bits. The longer codes fill fewer entries. Each 3-bit code fills two: r 0100 and 0101 , end 0110 and 0111 , 258 1000 and 1001 , 270 1010 and 1011 . Each 4-bit code fills one, ␣ to d , 1100 to 1111 . That makes 4 + 4 × 2 + 4 × 1 = 16: every entry is filled exactly once, so any 4 bits the decoder meets begin with exactly one code. Decode: peek at 4 bits. The data starts 0011 . Entry 0011 says a , 2 bits. The decoder outputs a and consumes only those 2 bits: the other two are the start of the next code. The window moves on by 2. The next 4 bits are 1101 : b , 4 bits. Output b , consume 4. 0100 : r , 3 bits. The decoder always peeks at 4 bits, and the entry says how far to move. It never needs to know in advance where a code ends. Four more lookups: 0011 a , 1110 c , 0011 a , 1111 d . Output so far: abracad . 1000 : 258, 3 bits. The last of those 4 bits isn't 258's: it was peeked at, not consumed. 258 is not a byte but a copy length, 4, so a distance comes next, looked up in the distance table, built the same way from the block's two distance lengths: 0 is d5, and its extra bit, 0 , makes the distance 7. The decoder copies abra from 7 bytes back: abracadabra . So a decoder builds its lookup tables itself, at the start of every dynamic block, from the lengths the block sends. It builds three, in order: first the code-length code's table, from its 3-bit lengths, so it can read the other lengths; then the literal/length and distance tables, from those. Only then can it read the data. A new dynamic block brings new lengths, so the tables are built again. A fixed block's lengths never change, "},{"id":"dynamic-header","heading":"Sending a dynamic code","text":"A dynamic block must tell the decoder its codes before its data. Thanks to canonical codes , it only needs to send each symbol's code length: 286 literal/length symbols and 30 distance symbols, 316 lengths in all, each from 0 to 15. The question is how to send them cheaply, and it matters: every bit spent on the code comes out of the saving. For the paragraph, the fitted code saved 854 bits on the data, and sending it cost 352 of them. Here are four tries, each fixing the biggest waste in the one before, measured on two blocks: the worked example written as a dynamic block (the one walked through below ), and zlib's real block for the 381-byte paragraph. Notice what the four steps do to the same list: the long bar is almost all zeros, cutting its end barely shortens it, and only collapsing the zeros into run symbols shrinks it. The bar is the cost to scale, against a mark at the example's 41 bits of data: even at its best, the code costs more than the data it describes. The strip under it shows what each step's cost is made of, as shares of that step, with the arithmetic under each part. Each chip is one code-length symbol; under it is what it stands for (×32 means a run of 32 zeros), its code once it has one, a run's extra bits as they arrive, and its cost in bits. Step 1: every slot, 4 bits. All 316 of the example's code lengths, in symbol order. Only the 11 marked slots are not zero. 1,264 bits. Step 2: say how many. HLIT and HDIST cut each list after its last used symbol, 270 and d6, so 38 slots go. 1,122 bits: the zeros in the middle are still there. Step 3: runs. Each stretch of zeros collapses into one run symbol: 18 for 11–138 zeros, 17 for 3–10. Each used length becomes one symbol too. The fans show which stretch of the old list each chip replaced. Read each column down. Symbol sent is what goes in the stream; stands for is what it means (×32 is a run of 32 zeros, a is a 's length); extra bits follow a run symbol and give its count as a plain number, least significant bit first, counted from the shortest run the symbol allows. Under the first 18, 1010100 read backwards is 0010101 , 21, and 18's runs start at 11, so 11 + 21 = 32. bits is what it costs: 5 for the symbol, plus its extra bits, so an 18 costs 5 + 7. 19 symbols come to 146 bits. Step 4: code the run symbols. 18, 3 and 4 get 2-bit codes, 17 gets 3 bits, 1 gets 4, and 0 and 2 get 5. With the 54 bits that describe this code, that makes 159 bits: for this short list, 13 more than step 3."},{"id":"step-naive","heading":"1. Every length, 4 bits each","text":"Write all 316 lengths as plain 4-bit numbers, in symbol order. That is 1,264 bits, 158 bytes, in every dynamic block whatever it holds. The example's data is only 41 bits, so its code table would cost 30 times as much as the data it describes. Even the paragraph's data, 1,521 bits, would nearly double. It is also more than ten times the tree's 111 bits for the example: the tree pays about 11 bits for each symbol a block uses, but the list pays 4 bits for every symbol it could use. Steps 2 to 4 cut what the unused ones cost."},{"id":"step-counts","heading":"2. Say how many","text":"Any one block uses few of the symbols. So send two counts first, HLIT and HDIST (plain 5-bit numbers), and stop each list after its highest used symbol; the rest count as 0. It barely helps: 1,122 bits for the example and 1,158 for the paragraph. The unused symbols are mostly in the middle of the alphabet, the byte values a text never contains, not at the end. The counts stay in the final scheme anyway: with no separators, they are how the decoder knows where each list ends."},{"id":"dynamic-runs","heading":"Runs, and a code for the runs","text":""},{"id":"step-runs","heading":"3. Replace runs with one symbol","text":"Write the lengths with a small alphabet of 19 symbols instead: 0–15 for a length, 16 to repeat the previous length, and 17 and 18 for runs of zeros, each followed by plain extra bits that give the count. For now, write each of these symbols as a plain 5-bit number. A whole stretch of unused byte values becomes one symbol: the example's 262 unused literal/length symbols take just 7. The header drops to 146 bits for the example ( step 3 in the figure ) and 443 for the paragraph. That is the big win, but the paragraph's header is still 443 bits. The code-length alphabet. Fixed by the format: the same in every dynamic block. Each range follows from its extra bits: 2 bits give 4 counts, 3 give 8, 7 give 128. Symbol Means Extra bits Count 0–15 a code length of 0 to 15 0 16 repeat the previous length 2 3–6 times 17 a run of zeros 3 3–10 18 a run of zeros 7 11–138 Runs start at 3 because a run of 1 or 2 is cheaper written out. Zeros get two run symbols, a short one and a long one, because they are by far the most common length."},{"id":"step-coded","heading":"4. Huffman-code the run symbols","text":"Step 3 still spends 5 bits on every run symbol, yet they are far from equally common. Of the paragraph's 78, the lengths 8 and 5 appear 14 times each and 0 appears 12 times, while 2 appears once and 16 not at all. That is the same skew Huffman coding removes from the data, one level down, so DEFLATE gives these symbols a Huffman code too: the code-length code , a Huffman code whose job is to send the other two codes. Its own 19 lengths go first, as plain 3-bit numbers. A dynamic block is therefore a stack of three layers, each written with the one below it: The three layers of a dynamic block, from the top. A decoder reads them from the bottom up. Layer Written with Size the data the literal/length and distance codes the whole block those two codes' lengths the code-length code up to 316 lengths the code-length code's lengths plain 3-bit numbers at most 19 × 3 = 57 bits It stops at three layers because the bottom one is so small: 57 bits at most, too little for yet another code to repay its own description. This is DEFLATE's actual scheme, and it costs 159 bits for the example ( step 4 in the figure ) and 352 for the paragraph. For the paragraph, coding the run symbols saved another 91 bits. For the tiny example it cost 13 more: describing the small code took more than it saved on just 19 run symbols. DEFLATE has no way to stop at step 3, which is one more reason tiny blocks are written with the fixed codes. And against the tree? For the example, the tree still wins, 111 bits to 159: a block with only 11 different symbols has too few names to be worth dropping. For the paragraph, which uses 46 literal/length symbols and 11 distances, the tree would cost 581 bits, 469 of them names, against 352 for the lengths. Real blocks run to thousands of symbols and use far more of the alphabet, so the gap only grows. Step The example The paragraph The tree, to beat 111 581 1. 4 bits each 1264 1264 2. Say how many 1122 1158 3. Runs 146 443 4. Code the runs 159 352 Runs make the big difference. Only after them does the list beat the tree, and only for the paragraph: the 35-byte example uses too few symbols, and its header ends up bigger than its data either way. What changes from block to block is only which of the 19 symbols a block uses, and the code each one gets: in the worked example below, symbol 18 is 10 and 16 isn't used at all, but 18 still means 11–138 zeros with 7 extra bits. For the 381-byte paragraph, zlib's whole header ( HLIT = 270, HDIST = 17, HCLEN = 16, then the lengths) is 352 bits, about 44 bytes. That is the price of the table, and the paragraph is long enough to repay it. The code-length code's 19 lengths are sent in a fixed order, 16, 17, 18, 0, 8, 7, 9, 6, 10, 5, 11, 4, 12, 3, 13, 2, 14, 1, 15, and HCLEN (4 bits) says how many are sent; any not sent count as 0. Go deeper: why that order? The order is a bet on which code lengths a block will use, most likely first, so that HCLEN can cut off the unlikely ones. The RFC lists it without a reason, but it reads in four parts: 16, 17, 18 : the repeat and zero-run symbols, which nearly every dynamic block uses to skip the byte values it never sees. 0 : every block has unused symbols. 8, 7, 9, 6, 10, 5, 11, 4, 12, 3, 13, 2, 14, 1 : outward from 8, because with 286 symbols most codes come out around 7–9 bits, and very short or very long codes are rare. 15 last, the rarest of all. For the paragraph, zlib's codes use lengths 2 to 8, so HCLEN is 16 and the last three, 14, 1 and 15, are never sent."},{"id":"dynamic-block","heading":"The same tokens as a dynamic block","text":"zlib wrote a fixed block for the opening string. To see every part of a dynamic Huffman block, here are the same tokens written as one by this page's encoder: it is the \"dynamic\" choice under Write it as . Dynamic means the codes are built for this one block, from its own counts, and sent at its start. They don't change while the block is read. The 26 bytes are 75 c6 31 15 00 00 08 42 c1 2a 56 fb 6a 02 fa 0f 3c 02 30 dd b1 e2 f8 30 e5 06 . This encoder's parse uses the copies from the window figure , (4, 7) and (23, 12), rather than zlib's (3, 7) and (22, 12). This is step 4 in full. It has the codes for the data, and the code-length code that sends their lengths. Keep the levels apart, because the walk below sends them bottom-up, the code-length code first: The three alphabets, and where the walk below defines and uses each one's code. The alphabets are the same in every dynamic block; the symbols used and their codes are this block's. Level Alphabet Symbols used in this block Their codes in this block Code defined in lines Code used in lines the data literal/length 9: a , b , r , c , d , ␣, 256, 258, 270 2, 3 or 4 bits 24–39 43–55 the data distance 2: d5, d6 1 bit each 40–42 51, 54 the lengths code-length (CL) 7: 1, 2, 3, 4 (the lengths above), 0, 17, 18 (for zeros) 2 to 5 bits 6–23 24–42 1 BFINAL = 1: last block 01 BTYPE: 10 = 2, dynamic 01110 HLIT: 01110 = 14, + 257 = 271 01100 HDIST: 00110 = 6, + 1 = 7 0111 HCLEN: 1110 = 14, + 4 = 18 000 CL symbol 16: 000 = 0, unused 110 CL symbol 17: 011 = 3 bits 010 CL symbol 18: 010 = 2 bits 101 CL symbol 0: 101 = 5 bits 000 CL symbol 8: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 7: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 9: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 6: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 10: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 5: 000 = 0, unused 000 CL symbol 11: 000 = 0, unused 010 CL symbol 4: 010 = 2 bits 000 CL symbol 12: 000 = 0, unused 010 CL symbol 3: 010 = 2 bits 000 CL symbol 13: 000 = 0, unused 101 CL symbol 2: 101 = 5 bits 000 CL symbol 14: 000 = 0, unused 001 CL symbol 1: 100 = 4 bits 10 +1010100 18: 32 zeros, 0–31 01 4: ␣ (32) gets 4 bits 10 +1010110 18: 64 zeros, 33–96 11111 2: a (97) gets 2 bits 01 4: b (98) gets 4 bits 01 4: c (99) gets 4 bits 01 4: d (100) gets 4 bits 10 +0100000 18: 13 zeros, 101–113 00 3: r (114) gets 3 bits 10 +1111111 18: 138 zeros, 115–252 110 +000 17: 3 zeros, 253–255 00 3: 256 (end) gets 3 bits 11110 0: 257 unused 00 3: 258 gets 3 bits 10 +0000000 18: 11 zeros, 259–269 00 3: 270 gets 3 bits 110 +010 17: 5 zeros, d0–d4 1110 1: d5 gets 1 bit 1110 1: d6 gets 1 bit 00 literal a (97) 1101 literal b (98) 010 literal r (114) 00 literal a (97) 1110 literal c (99) 00 literal a (97) 1111 literal d (100) 100 length symbol 258 → 4 0 +0 distance d5 → 7 1100 literal ␣ (32) 101 +00 length symbol 270 → 23 1 +11 distance d6 → 12 011 end of block (256) 00000 padding (not read) The block header. BFINAL is 1. BTYPE arrives as 0 , then 1 : binary 10, a dynamic block. Every code from here on is defined by the block itself. How many lengths follow. The code lengths come next as a bare list in symbol order: the first is symbol 0's, the second symbol 1's, with no symbol numbers and nothing to mark where the list ends and the data begins. So the block says first how long each list is. HLIT : 271 literal/length lengths, for symbols 0–270. 270 is the highest symbol this block uses, so the encoder stops there and leaves out 271–285. HDIST : 7 distance lengths, d0–d6. HCLEN : 18 of the 19 code-length-code lengths. The names are the RFC's: they count the lit eral/length, dist ance and c ode- len gth codes. The RFC never says what the H stands for, though it is usually read as Huffman. All three are plain numbers, which arrive lowest bit first, so each line shows the bits the usual way round as well: 0111 arrives, read backwards that is 1110 = 14, and HCLEN stores the count minus 4. The code-length code. CL is short for code length : the code-length alphabet has 19 symbols, 0–18, used to write out the code"},{"id":"choosing","heading":"Choosing a block type","text":"An encoder now has a choice for every block, and it matters. The example's dynamic block is twice the size of its fixed one, while the paragraph's dynamic block is a fifth smaller than its fixed one. And for data with no repetition or skew, such as random bytes, both codes make things worse: the fixed code spends 8 or 9 bits on every byte. That is what the third type, the stored block, is for: it copies the bytes as they are. Here are the three side by side. Stored (00) No compression: up to 65,535 raw bytes after a 4-byte length and its complement. Caps the cost of incompressible data at 5 bytes per 64 KiB No saving at all Fixed Huffman (01) Codes set by the RFC: 8 or 9 bits for a literal, 7 or 8 for a length, 5 for a distance. No table to send Wins on short inputs Ignores this data's skew Dynamic Huffman (10) Codes built for this block's own counts, sent as code lengths at the start of the block. Removes the skew too Usually wins once a block holds more than a few dozen bytes of text The table costs tens of bytes Any input can be written as any of the three, and every one is valid: a decoder accepts whichever it gets. Which one wins is a sum, not a rule. The encoder works out the exact size of each kind for every block (zlib ends a block after about 16,000 symbols) and writes the smallest. Tiny blocks come out fixed because a code table costs more than it saves: for the paragraph in the chart, zlib 1.2.12 switches from fixed to dynamic once the text is 74 bytes long. Input Stored Fixed Dynamic zlib -9 abracadabra ×3 (35 B) 114.3 37.1 74.3 42.9 A paragraph (381 B) 101.3 78.2 62.2 61.7 Random bytes (1 KiB) 100.5 105.6 105.2 100.5 Each input has a different winner. The short string can't pay for a table; the paragraph can; random bytes can't be compressed at all, so storing them is cheapest. The first three columns are the same tokens written as each block type; zlib picks per block."},{"id":"not-symbols","heading":"Everything in the stream that isn't a symbol","text":"Every part of a stream has now appeared, but scattered across the sections above, each one introduced where it was needed. To read a stream yourself, or to write a decoder, you need them in one place, with one rule for how each is written. A DEFLATE stream is mostly Huffman codes, but not entirely. Bits are written in two ways, and a decoder must know which kind comes next: A Huffman code is read one bit at a time, most significant bit first, because the decoder walks down the code tree as each bit arrives. Everything else is a plain number of a fixed, known width, read lowest bit first. Each byte of the stream fills from its lowest bit up, so a plain number's bits arrive in the order they are packed. In other words, DEFLATE is little-endian throughout, at the level of bits and of bytes (a stored block's 16-bit LEN comes low byte first), except that Huffman codes are stored bit-reversed. Write a byte the usual way, most significant bit on the left, and its plain fields read as ordinary binary, stacked from the right. Here is the first byte of the dynamic block above: 0 1 1 1 0 | 1 0 | 1 HLIT | BTYPE | BFINAL = 14 | = 2 | = 1 A Huffman code reads correctly in arrival order instead, so in this byte view it would look reversed. Decoders such as zlib reverse each code once, when they build their lookup tables, so that one little-endian bit reader can handle everything. Every plain field in a DEFLATE stream. None of these is Huffman-coded: the decoder knows each one's width from what came before it. Field Bits Where Holds BFINAL 1 start of every block 1 if this is the last block BTYPE 2 after BFINAL the block type ( values ) length extra bits 0–5 after a length symbol which length within the symbol's range: 270 means lengths 23–26, and its 2 extra bits, 0–3, add the offset ( try one ) distance extra bits 0–13 after a distance symbol which distance within the symbol's range: d6 means distances 9–12, and its 2 extra bits add the offset, so 11 = 3 gives 12 HLIT 5 dynamic block, after BTYPE the number of literal/length code lengths, minus 257: 257–288 is 32 values HDIST 5 after HLIT the number of distance code lengths, minus 1: 1–32 is 32 values HCLEN 4 after HDIST the number of code-length-code lengths, minus 4: 4–19 is 16 values code-length-code lengths 3 each after HCLEN , 4–19 of them the lengths of the code that compresses the code lengths, in the fixed order; that code's lengths are at most 7 run extra bits 2, 3 or 7 after code-length symbol 16, 17 or 18 the run's count, minus 3, 3 or 11 alignment 0–7 stored block, after BTYPE nothing: skipped to reach the next byte boundary LEN , NLEN 16 each stored block, after the alignment the number of bytes, then its ones' complement as a check stored bytes 8 each stored block, after NLEN the data itself, byte for byte final padding 0–7 after the last block nothing: fills the last byte Everything else in the stream is a Huffman code: the literal/length and distance symbols, and, inside a dynamic block's header, the code-length symbols. The worked example's 119 bits show both kinds side by side in the bit walk . Nothing in the stream says how long a field is. The decoder always knows what comes next and how big it is, in one of three ways: A width fixed by the RFC , chosen to fit the field's range: BFINAL , BTYPE , HLIT , HDIST , HCLEN , the 3-bit code-length-code lengths, and a stored block's LEN and NLEN . After BTYPE = 2, the next 5 bits are HLIT because the format says so. A width set by the symbol just decoded : the extra bits. Symbol 270 is always followed by 2 extra bits, d6 by 2, run symbol 18 by 7. No width at all : Huffman codes end themselves. The decoder reads one bit at a time until the bits so far match a code, and since no code is the start of another, it can never stop in the wrong place. That is why the stream needs no separators or length prefixes, and also why it must be read strictly in order: where HDIST starts depends on having read HLIT , and one corrupted bit misalign"},{"id":"try-it","heading":"Try it","text":"You have seen both savings on one 35-byte string. Here they are on any text you like. Type anything, or pick an example. The tokens are what LZ77 found; the numbers show each saving on its own and both together. Every count is the size of a real DEFLATE stream that this page's encoder writes, not an estimate. Text to compress 35 bytes abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra ×3 A paragraph One letter ×300 No repeats 280 bits as plain bytes 223 bits with Huffman alone 103 bits with LZ77 alone (fixed codes) 203 bits with both, incl. a 159 -bit table 13 bytes out, as a fixed block Write it as the smallest stored fixed dynamic 13 bytes ( 103 bits) as a fixed block: 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 01 51 0a 38 d8 00 LZ77 replaced 27 of 35 bytes with 2 back-references, leaving 8 literals. Codes built for this text would need a 159-bit table, more than they save, so the fixed codes win. For comparison, your browser's own DEFLATE ( CompressionStream ) wrote … bytes. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Click a back-reference to see the bytes it copies. a b r a c a d (4,7) ␣ (23,12) The token stream as a table # Token Stands for At byte 1 a a 0 2 b b 1 3 r r 2 4 a a 3 5 c c 4 6 a a 5 7 d d 6 8 (4,7) abra 7 9 ␣ ␣ 11 10 (23,12) abracadabra abracadabra 12 Click any back-reference to see which earlier bytes it copies, and use Write it as to see the same tokens as each kind of block: the smallest is what an encoder would send, but every one decodes. The paragraph shows both savings clearly: LZ77 finds the repeated words, and the skew of English letters pays for a dynamic table. One letter ×300 is almost all repetition. \"No repeats\" has nothing for LZ77 and too little text to pay for a table, so it comes out slightly larger than it went in."},{"id":"serial","heading":"Why decoding is serial","text":"Everything so far has been about size. The price of that density is speed. The output has no separators and no index. A code's length is known only once it has been decoded, so the decoder cannot find where symbol n + 1 starts without decoding symbol n . Back-references add a second chain: a copy reads bytes that earlier tokens wrote. That is why a gzip file is decompressed by one core, front to back, however many cores the machine has. Getting around it, for example to decode on a GPU, means finding where blocks start without decoding what comes before, and resolving copies whose sources aren't decoded yet. A later series here takes that on; nothing on this page depends on it."},{"id":"edge-cases","heading":"Edge cases","text":"Overlapping copies Allowed and common: distance 1 with length 258 repeats the previous byte 258 times. A decoder must copy byte by byte, not with a block copy, or it reads bytes that haven't been written yet. The longest match 258 bytes, with its own symbol (285) and no extra bits, so with the fixed codes a long run costs 13 bits per 258 bytes. 258 = 255 + 3: a length fits in a byte once the minimum of 3 is subtracted. End of block Symbol 256 ends every Huffman block, even the last. It needs a code, so it counts as a symbol when the tree is built. Empty input zlib writes 2 bytes, 03 00 : a final fixed block holding only the end-of-block code. Incompressible input Stored blocks cap the overhead at 5 bytes per 65,535. A large file of random bytes grows by under 0.01%; the 1 KiB in the chart grows by 0.5%."},{"id":"terms","heading":"Terms used on this page","text":"These are defined here, for this page. DEFLATE's broader terms are in the site glossary : DEFLATE , LZ77 , Huffman code , canonical Huffman code , back-reference , sliding window , end-of-block and extra bits . Alphabet The fixed set of symbols a Huffman code is built over. DEFLATE uses three: literal/length, distance and code-length. How the two ideas join BFINAL The first bit of every block: 1 if it is the last block of the stream. Blocks, and the fixed code BTYPE The 2 bits after BFINAL that give the block type: 00 stored, 01 fixed Huffman codes, 10 dynamic Huffman codes. 11 is invalid. Like every plain number, they arrive lowest bit first. The values Code length The number of bits in a symbol's Huffman code. 0 means the symbol has no code. A dynamic block sends only the lengths; the codes follow from them by the canonical rule. Send lengths, not trees Code-length code The small Huffman code a dynamic block uses to write down its code lengths. Its alphabet has 19 symbols: 0–15 are lengths, 16 repeats the previous length, and 17 and 18 are runs of zeros. CL is short for code length. Huffman-code the run symbols Distance alphabet The 30 symbols, d0–d29, that stand for back-reference distances from 1 to 32,768. Most stand for a range, picked out by extra bits. It has a Huffman code of its own. How the two ideas join Dynamic Huffman code Codes built for one block from that block's own symbol counts, and sent as code lengths at its start ( BTYPE 10). They don't change while the block is read: dynamic means per block, not adaptive. A dynamic block, bit by bit Fixed Huffman code Codes set once by the RFC, for any block with BTYPE 01, so no table is sent: 8 or 9 bits per literal, 7 or 8 per length symbol, 5 per distance symbol. Why 7–9 bits HCLEN 4 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many code-length-code lengths follow, minus 4. Those lengths come in a fixed order, so the count can cut off the usually-unused ones at the end. Why that order HDIST 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many distance code lengths follow, minus 1. In the bit walk HLIT 5 bits in a dynamic block's header: how many literal/length code lengths follow, minus 257. The lengths come as a bare list in symbol order, so the count says where the list ends. In the bit walk Literal A byte that LZ77 sends as itself, because no match of 3 or more starts there. It becomes the literal/length symbol with the same value, 0–255. LZ77 Literal/length alphabet The 286 symbols 0–285: 0–255 for literal bytes, 256 for the end of the block, and 257–285 for ranges of lengths. One Huffman code covers them all, so a single code says whether a literal or a copy comes next. How the two ideas join Overlapping copy A back-reference whose length is greater than its distance, so it reads bytes it is itself writing. It works because the decoder copies one byte at a time; distance 1 simply repeats a byte. The bit walk shows one Plain number A field of fixed, known width that is not Huffman-coded, read lowest bit first: the block header, the extra bits, a dynamic block's counts and code-length-code lengths, and a stored block's LEN and NLEN . Every plain field Prefix code A code in which no codeword is the start of another, so codes can be read back to back without separators. Every Huffman code is a prefix code. Huffman Stored block A block of up to 65,535 raw bytes ( BTYPE 00), after a byte-aligned length and its complement. Encoders use it when compressing would make the data bigger. Choosing a block type Symbol One element of an alphabet: what Huffman coding counts and gives a code. DEFLATE maps LZ77's tokens onto symbols, so Huffman coding never sees the raw bytes. How the two ideas join Token One item of LZ77's output: a literal, or a back-reference (length, distance). Each becomes one or two symbols. LZ77"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"RFC 1951, DEFLATE : the format itself, short and readable. Section 3.2 has the alphabets and the canonical code. RFC 1952, gzip and RFC 1950, zlib : the two wrappers, a few header bytes and a checksum around the same stream. Huffman (1952), \"A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes\" : the original paper. Ziv and Lempel (1977), \"A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression\" : where LZ77 gets its name. zlib's algorithm.txt : how zlib's encoder chooses matches, which this page leaves out."}]},{"href":"docs/pedagogical-writing.html","title":"Pedagogical writing: so the reader could have invented it","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer Pedagogical writing: so the reader could have invented it Pedagogical writing is writing whose job is to teach: the reader should leave able to use an idea, not just able to say they have read it. Clarity is not enough for that. Here is what else it takes, built up from one clear paragraph that fails. Order an idea so the reader could have invented it: make them need it, try the obvious answer, show exactly where that answer breaks, then give the fix, and only then its name. Then ask a question that someone still holding the wrong answer would get wrong. It matters because clarity is not learning. In one physics study, a clear, correct video raised students' confidence just as much as a video that taught them twice as much. The common mistake is writing in the order you now understand the idea, answer first and reason after. That hides the question the answer was for, and leaves the reader's wrong model untouched. Already know the loop? The method in five lines is in the last section , and a routine for your next draft is in Your turn ."},{"id":"clear-is-not-enough","heading":"Clear, correct, and nothing learned","text":"A first-year student answers a physics question, watches a seven-minute video that explains the answer, and answers it wrong again, more confident than before. The question is about a book sliding down a rough ramp at constant speed: is the force pulling it down the ramp greater than the friction holding it back, less, or equal? She picks \"greater\", reasoning that something has to win for the book to keep moving. The right answer is \"equal\": at constant speed the forces balance. Then she watches a video that explains Newton's first and second laws with animations and live demonstrations, including a book pushed across a table at constant speed. Asked again, she answers \"greater\" again, and raises her confidence from 5 to 6 out of 7 \"because she believes she better understands the concepts after watching the video\". Notice that her picture has a winner, and a winner is what makes a book speed up. Sliding at constant speed needs no winner: the two arrows are the same length. Her model, that something has to win for the book to keep moving, is exactly what the video never touches. She is the opening example of a paper by Derek Muller and Manjula Sharma at the University of Sydney, drawn from their studies of more than a thousand physics students over three years. They introduce her as \"Imagine a first year student\", so she is a typical case from their data, not one named person. Muller later started the Veritasium channel. Nothing was wrong with the video. In their words, \"the sentences used to describe the phenomena are not long or convoluted, nor does the presenter seem to struggle with making the concepts plain.\" They compared that clear lecture with other versions of the same lesson. One, the Dialogue, was a scripted conversation in which a student says the common wrong ideas out loud and a tutor takes them apart. Another, the Refutation, had a single narrator voice and correct the same wrong ideas. Guess before you open this: which raised the students' confidence more, the clear lecture or the Dialogue that voiced their wrong ideas? The lecture, the Dialogue, or about the same? About the same. In the 2006 study every version raised students' confidence by 0.6 to 0.7 points on average, on the seven-point scale, with no significant difference between them. Learning was another matter: students who watched the Dialogue or the Refutation gained on average twice as much as those who watched the clear lecture. Notice that the confidence column is flat , so a reader's confidence can't tell you which version taught, while the learning column separates the two that put the wrong ideas on the table. The bars show the headline results, not raw scores: confidence at the middle of the reported range, learning as multiples of the lecture's gain. If you picked the lecture, you had a reason: it is the more polished explanation, and polish feels like teaching. If you picked the Dialogue, you expected confidence to follow learning. It doesn't. Confidence tracks how smoothly the material went down, and a clear video goes down smoothly whether or not it changes anything. A later experiment found the same from the other side: viewers of a clear video that taught misconceptions felt just as sure as viewers of a correct one, and came away with \"more misconceptions and fewer scientifically correct conceptions\" (Kulgemeyer and Wittwer, 2022). That gap between feeling taught and being taught is what this page is about. Clarity is necessary, but the ramp shows it is not sufficient: the reader also has to want the idea, meet their own wrong answer, and see where it breaks. So the question for this page is concrete. Take a clear, correct paragraph that a newcomer reads, feels good about, and cannot use. What do you change, and how do you find out whether it worked? Here is the route. The next two sections take one such paragraph, from Wikipedia, and find exactly where it fails a reader. Then we rebuild it in four steps, which I'll call beats, and switch"},{"id":"first-try","heading":"A first try: just say it clearly","text":"The obvious way to explain something is to say it clearly: state the rule, give the reason, name it, and get every detail right. This is how most of us write, and it rests on a model of reading worth stating in full. The reader takes in each sentence. If every sentence is true and clear and follows from the one before, the reader ends up holding what the writer holds. On this model, every failure to teach is a failure of clarity: a long sentence, an undefined term, a skipped step. So the cure is always more editing for clarity. Here is a paragraph that passes that test. It is from Wikipedia's article on the dynamic array, the growable array behind Rust's Vec , Java's ArrayList and Python's list . I chose it because it is good: short, true, and in a sensible order. To avoid incurring the cost of resizing many times, dynamic arrays resize by a large amount, such as doubling in size, and use the reserved space for future expansion. […] As n elements are inserted, the capacities form a geometric progression. Expanding the array by any constant proportion a ensures that inserting n elements takes O ( n ) time overall, meaning that each insertion takes amortized constant time (so long as any given allocation takes O (1) time). From Wikipedia, “Dynamic array”, section “Geometric expansion and amortized cost”, revision of 21 September 2026 , CC BY-SA 4.0 . […] marks an omitted code sample that doubles the capacity and copies. Read it once as a newcomer would. If you already know why arrays double, this reads well, and that turns out to be part of the problem. One fact the paragraph takes for granted: a resize allocates a new buffer and copies every item across. With that, here is a question the paragraph should let a newcomer answer. Commit to a guess before you open it; a guess is enough. A colleague suggests growing by a fixed 1,000 slots instead of doubling: less wasted space, and still \"a large amount\". Starting empty, a million appends copy roughly how many items in total? About 1 million, about 500 million, or about 500 billion? About 500 million , nearly 500 times what doubling copies. A copy happens every 1,000 appends and moves everything so far: 1,000 + 2,000 + … + 999,000, which is 1,000 × (1 + 2 + … + 999) = 499,500,000. Doubling copies only at 1, 2, 4, … items, up to 524,288 (2 19 ), 1,048,575 in all: about one copy per item. Appends Fixed step of 1,000 Doubling 100k 4.95 0.13 200k 19.9 0.26 300k 44.85 0.52 400k 79.8 0.52 500k 124.75 0.52 600k 179.7 1.05 700k 244.65 1.05 800k 319.6 1.05 900k 404.55 1.05 1,000k 499.5 1.05 Notice that the fixed step curves upward because every resize copies everything so far, while doubling stays on the floor and only steps up when the array fills at 2 k items. At a million appends the gap is 499.5 million against 1.05 million. \"About 1 million\" is the answer the paragraph's first sentence teaches: \"resize by a large amount\", and 1,000 is large. \"About 500 billion\" is the count for growing by one slot at a time, 1 + 2 + … + 999,999 items. That sum is about 999,999² ÷ 2, so it grows with the square of the number of appends. A step of 1,000 divides it by 1,000, and that is all it does: the total still grows with the square. Ten million appends would cost 100 times as much, not 10 times. Doubling is cheap because its step grows with the array."},{"id":"crack","heading":"Where clear writing cracks","text":"If you answered \"about 1 million\", the paragraph taught you exactly what it says first. The idea that rules out 1,000 is in it, but in the one place a reader is least likely to take it from. Let's look at where each part sits, and at what a reader takes from it. The answer comes before the question. The first sentence gives the rule, \"resize by a large amount, such as doubling\", for a problem stated only in the abstract: \"the cost of resizing many times\". The reader never sees that cost, so they have nothing to measure \"large\" against. The wrong answer is never tried. The natural first attempt, growing by a fixed step, never appears, so it is never broken. Worse, the first sentence half-endorses it, since 1,000 slots is \"a large amount\". The reader's own model leaves the paragraph exactly as it came in, as the ramp student's did. The idea is buried. What makes doubling work, \"any constant proportion\", sits inside the subject of the last sentence, among symbols, while the end of the sentence, where readers put their weight (the section on sentences says why), goes to a caveat in brackets. It is the one phrase that separates the right model from the wrong one. Remember the word proportion : we will come back to where it should have gone. None of these is a failure of clarity: each sentence is clear. The failure is the order. The paragraph runs in the order its author holds the idea, rule first and reason after, which is the reverse of the order a newcomer could build it in. Grant Sanderson, who makes the 3Blue1Brown maths videos on YouTube and has thought harder than most about why explanations work, gives the same diagnosis as advice: \"try very hard to structure your explanation to go from the concrete to the abstract. I think almost always when you understand something the natural inclination is to go the other way around.\" That inclination has a name, the curse of knowledge : once you know something, it is hard to imagine not knowing it. Steven Pinker calls it \"the single best explanation I know of why good people write bad prose.\" It is also why the paragraph may have read well to you. If you already hold the model, every gap fills itself in, so the gaps are invisible to the one person who could close them. So the crack points at what the fix must do, and better sentences are not it. The reader has to hold the question before the answer, and their own wrong answer before the right one, so that by the time the rule arrives they can nearly guess it."},{"id":"core-loop","heading":"The fix: the question before the answer","text":"This is the key step of the page. We rebuild the paragraph so that each part answers a question the part before it raised, and the rule arrives when you can nearly guess it. Below is the same content in both orders, Wikipedia's on top and my rewrite underneath. Step through it and watch what moves and what we have to add. Notice how little of the content changes. The rule becomes the fix , fourth instead of first; the buried reason becomes the crack's conclusion ; the name stays last . What the rewrite adds, the dashed boxes, is a question, a wrong answer and a check . Arrows mean \"becomes\". Both orders at once. Wikipedia's paragraph is on top, the rewrite underneath, and each arrow shows where a part of the original ended up. The steps take the rewrite one beat at a time. Wikipedia's order: rule, reason, name. Every sentence is true, and each follows from the last. Nothing in it is a question, and nothing in it is wrong. Two beats with no counterpart. A question with a stake (a million appends: how much copying?) and the answer a newcomer is most likely to give: grow by a fixed 1,000 slots. It is labelled as a first try, so a skimmer doesn't keep it. The crack counts what the attempt costs , 500 million copies, and finds the waste: every resize copies the early items again. So the room added has to grow with the array. That is Wikipedia's \"any constant proportion\", promoted from a clause to the conclusion of an argument. Only now the rule: double it. It moves from first to fourth, where the reader can nearly guess it, because the crack has said what it must do. The name is last in both. Wikipedia gets this right. In the rewrite the name labels counts the reader has just seen: a million appends, about a million copies. A check the reader can fail. Growing by 1.5× is a near-twin of the 1,000-slot question: same idea, different surface. A reader who took away \"double\" rather than \"in proportion\" calls it quadratic, and finds out they were wrong. The figure has six boxes, but only four beats. The name belongs to the fix, which ends on it, and the check comes after the explanation rather than inside it; it gets a section of its own . I'll call the four beats together the core loop , because on a teaching page every idea goes round it once: Need. Make the reader want the idea, with a puzzle, a goal or a failure, and put numbers in it: a million appends, how much copying? Naive attempt. Try the approach your reader would try, concretely, and label it as a first try where it appears, so a skimmer doesn't leave with it: a fixed step of 1,000 slots. Crack. Show where it breaks, and exactly where: which case, which term. Phrase the break as the property any fix must have: every resize copies the early items again, so the room added must grow with the array. Fix, then name. Give the fix in the words of that property, then its technical name with its plain meaning: double it; that is amortised O(1). Each beat does a different job. The need is what keeps a reader reading; Sanderson argues that education \"is not rate limited by the clarity of explanation, it's rate limited by motivation.\" The naive attempt and the crack do what Muller's Dialogue added to the clear lecture: the reader's wrong model is said out loud, as a mechanism, then broken on a case. On the ramp, that addition doubled the learning gains. And the name comes last because, as Sanderson says of notation in a good explanation, \"it arrives only once it's articulating something that already exists at least loosely in the viewer's mind.\" A caution about the word \"invented\". People left to rediscover an idea on their own learn less than people who are told it: a meta-analysis of 580 comparisons found unassisted discovery behind explicit instruction (d = −0.38), while discovery with scaffolding, worked examples and feedback came out ahead (d = +0.30). d is an effect size: the gap between two groups' average scores, in standard deviations. 0.3 is a modest but real difference. Alfie"},{"id":"beats","heading":"What each beat does","text":"\"Each beat matters\" is easy to nod along to. Let's test it instead: switch a beat off and read what is left. Below is the rewrite in full, each paragraph tagged with its beat. Read it once as it stands. Then switch off the Crack and read the fix again; the note under the text says what a newcomer would now be missing. The other switches work the same way, with two twists: Name last off moves the name to the top, and with the naive attempt off the crack goes too, since there is nothing left to break. Rule and name only strips the text back to Wikipedia's shape. Switches Need Naive attempt Crack Name last Check All beats Rule and name only Name, moved first A dynamic array gets amortised O(1) appends by growing in proportion. Need A dynamic array, like Rust's Vec or Python's list , never asks how long it will get. Its items sit in one contiguous buffer, and a buffer can't grow in place: when it is full, the array must allocate a bigger one and copy every item across. Append a million log lines to one, and how much copying is that? Naive attempt A first try: grow by a fixed step, say 1,000 slots at a time. Then only one append in 1,000 has to copy anything. Crack Count the copies. Each resize moves everything so far: 1,000 items, then 2,000, then 3,000. A million appends copy 1,000 + 2,000 + … + 999,000 items: about 500 million. A bigger step only divides that total, because the early items are still copied again at every resize. So whatever we change must make resizes rarer as the array grows: the room we add has to grow with the array. Fix So grow in proportion: when the buffer is full, double it. Copies now happen only at 1, 2, 4, 8, … items, and each one buys as many free appends as it moved. A million appends copy 1 + 2 + 4 + … + 524,288 items: about a million, roughly one copy per item, and nearly 500 times fewer than the fixed step. Name That is what amortised O(1) means: n appends cost work proportional to n , so the average append costs a constant amount, even though one unlucky append still copies half a million items. Check Java's ArrayList grows by half its length each time, not by double. Is that still a constant number of copies per item, or does the total grow with the square, like the fixed step's? Still a constant per item: the room it adds, half the array, grows with the array. Counting from ArrayList 's default 10 slots, a million appends copy about 2.4 million items. That is more than doubling's million, and nowhere near the fixed step's 500 million. Doubling was never the point; proportion is. Every beat on. Each paragraph answers the question the one before it raised, and the check tests the one idea a reader is most likely to get wrong. Switch a beat off to see what a newcomer loses. Need off. The text now opens on an answer to a question nobody asked: why would anyone grow anything by 1,000 slots? \"Each resize moves everything so far\" refers to copying the reader was never told about, and 500 million has nothing to be large next to. Naive attempt off, and the crack with it: with no wrong answer on the table, there is nothing to break. \"Double it\" arrives as a rule handed down, which is the shape of the Wikipedia paragraph. Crack off. The fixed step is proposed and never shown to fail, and \"So grow in proportion\" now follows from nothing. Why double, and not keep the 1,000-slot step just offered? The property that rules it out, room that grows with the array, was in the crack. A reader who guessed \"about 1 million\" earlier would still think they were right. Name first. The reader meets \"amortised O(1)\" before any count it could summarise, so it is a label with nothing to stick to. It also gives away the answer before the need has asked the question. Check off. Nothing tells a reader who took away \"double\" rather than \"in proportion\" that they did; they would call Java's 1.5× quadratic. They finish as confident as the student on the ramp. Rule and name only: the Wikipedia paragraph's shape. Every sentence le"},{"id":"ladder","heading":"But a page holds many ideas: a ladder","text":"So far, one idea and one loop. A real page has several, and how we join their loops decides whether the page reads as a lesson or as a list. The rewrite already shows the join. Doubling has a flaw of its own: right after a resize, up to half the new buffer is empty. That flaw is the next loop's need. Its fix is a smaller factor, such as Java's 1.5 or CPython's roughly 1.125 (Wikipedia's article has a table of them), which trades more copies for less empty space while keeping the room growing with the array. And that fix has its own flaw: every resize still copies the whole array in one go, a pause that a program with a latency budget notices, and the need for a further loop. Each fix leaves a problem, and each problem starts the next loop, so the joins between loops are always \"but\" or \"therefore\", never \"and then\". Nicky Case borrowed this rule for interactive explanations from Matt Stone and Trey Parker, who write stories not as \"this happens, and then that happens\", but as \"this happens, THEREFORE that happens, BUT that happens, THEREFORE this happens\". A page in this blog's series on DEFLATE, the compression inside gzip and PNG, shows a whole ladder. You don't need to know DEFLATE to follow its shape. A compressor that has invented its own short codes for a stretch of data must first tell the decoder what they are. It does that by sending a list of 316 small numbers, one for every symbol the format allows, and most of them are 0 because most symbols never occur. The question is how to send that list cheaply. How a DEFLATE block sends its own code takes four tries, each measured in bits on the same 381-byte paragraph of English, and each fixing the biggest waste the one before it left: Notice that each crack, in italics, is what the next try fixes, and that one try barely helps: cutting the tail saves 106 bits , because the zeros are in the middle of the list. The page keeps that try anyway, since the counts it adds tell the decoder how many numbers to read. Bars are to scale; the numbers are the page's own measurements. Three habits keep your ladder climbable. One new idea per rung : a section that needs a second mechanism is two sections. Measure every rung on one benchmark , as the bits above are, so the gap that remains is visibly the next need. And raise the next problem before the reader does . Just before the ladder above, that page swaps a description naming only the 11 symbols its worked example used for a list with an entry for all 316. It objects to its own move straight away: \"You might object that we have swapped 11 symbol names for something worse: a length for every symbol a block could use, 316 of them, used or not. You would be right, and squeezing that list is the rest of this page.\" A reader who was about to object learns that the writer saw it too, and why to keep reading. Not every attempt deserves a loop of its own. A dead end that teaches nothing gets one sentence; show in full only the attempt whose crack points somewhere."},{"id":"promises","heading":"When a step can't be guessed: promises","text":"But not every rung can be made guessable yet. You may need a simplification for now, a \"why\" that only makes sense later, or a detail that would derail the story. The loop doesn't cover these steps. A promise does. A promise is any place where you ask the reader to accept something on credit. Promises are fine. Unflagged or unpaid ones lose readers, because the reader can't tell a temporary simplification from the truth, and a skimmer keeps the simplification. Three moves handle them: Flag it where you make it, with its size. Sanderson, simplifying a quantum algorithm: \"You should know there is that added bit of complexity that I'm postponing, but this is 90% of the right idea.\" The reader now knows what kind of claim they are holding. Pay it off by name. Come back to the claim, quote it, correct it, and say what the simple version would have got wrong. At its third try, the DEFLATE page writes each run symbol (a short code standing for a run of zeros) \"for now … as a plain 5-bit number\", and the next section opens on exactly that cost: \"Step 3 still spends 5 bits on every run symbol, yet they are far from equally common.\" Defer out loud. Name the question the reader is about to ask, say where it is answered (or that it isn't), and give them the one fact they need to carry on. Claims need the same honesty. For each statement the page doesn't show, say where it stands: shown later, cited, measured, or only asserted. And treat every \"obviously\" as a claim with no status: either show it, or say where it stands. I have made promises on this page too. \"For now, treat a page as a single loop\" was paid off in the last section. The word proportion is still owed; it is paid in the section on sentences ."},{"id":"checks","heading":"Smooth reading isn't learning: make the reader commit","text":"Even a perfect loop can be read without being learned, which is exactly what happened on the ramp. The only way you or the reader can find out is a question they can get wrong. You have answered two on this page, and that was deliberate. A meta-analysis of questions asked before a lesson, with its methods registered before the analysis, found that they improved learning of the material they asked about (g = 0.54) but barely touched the rest (g = 0.04). They helped far more when people guessed an answer (g = 0.65) than when they only read the question (g = 0.22). g is the same kind of effect size as d . St. Hilaire, Chan and Ahn, 2024. So ask for a commitment, an option, a number or a shape, and say that a guess is enough. Aim it at the key step, because the benefit stays where the question points. The harder part is choosing which question to ask. A question teaches only if the reader's likely wrong model gives a different answer from the right one. Here are two questions we could have asked about the Wikipedia paragraph: Is doubling better than adding one slot at a time? Both models answer yes. A reader who thinks \"grow by a large amount\" gets it right Says nothing about proportion Grow by 1,000 slots: about how many copies? The models disagree. \"A large amount\" predicts about 1 million; \"proportion\" predicts about 500 million A wrong guess tells the reader which model they hold Build each wrong option from one misconception, and in the reveal say why each was a reasonable guess before correcting it, as the 1,000-slot reveal did. Then state the insight, not only the number, so a reader who was right for the wrong reason finds out. When a reader meets a mechanism more than once, fade the support: work the first case fully, leave the last step of the second as a prediction, and give the third to the reader. This page does that with the loop itself. Wikipedia's paragraph was worked through for you, the DEFLATE ladder came with its cracks labelled, and the exercise is yours."},{"id":"sentences","heading":"The same order inside a sentence","text":"The loop puts what the reader already holds before what is new. A good sentence does the same, on a smaller scale. George Gopen and Judith Swan found that readers expect a sentence to open with old information that links back, and to end on the new information it exists to deliver. They call the end of a sentence its stress position , because readers give the most weight to whatever comes just before the full stop. Their rule: \"Put in the topic position the old information that links backward; put in the stress position the new information you want the reader to emphasize.\" Now we can pay off proportion . Wikipedia's last sentence puts that word near the start, inside the subject, and gives its stress position to a caveat in brackets. In both versions below, the load-bearing word and the end of each sentence are marked: As written “Expanding the array by any constant proportion a ensures that inserting n elements takes O ( n ) time overall, meaning that each insertion takes amortized constant time (so long as any given allocation takes O (1) time) .” Stress where it belongs “Inserting n elements then takes O ( n ) time overall, amortised constant time per insertion . What makes that true is that the array grows by a constant proportion, not by a constant amount .” The second version adds no facts. It reorders them so that each sentence ends on its point, and the last one ends on the contrast a newcomer is most likely to miss: proportion, not amount. That is the 1,000-slot question, answered in the stress position. (The caveat about allocation is true but secondary; it can go in its own sentence or a footnote.) Two more habits come from the same idea: Keep one story per paragraph. List the first few words of each sentence. They should name the same character, or follow a chain in which each sentence starts where the last one ended. If they read \"The array… Copies… A factor… Memory…\", the paragraph has no subject, and the reader has to build the links alone. One name per thing. A new word reads as a new thing. If \"buffer\", \"allocation\" and \"storage\" all mean the same memory, the reader counts three things. Joseph Williams, in his textbook Style , warns against this \"elegant variation\". It is why this page calls the beats need, naive attempt, crack and fix every time."},{"id":"figures","heading":"Figures and interactives earn their place","text":"Order fixes most of what goes wrong in prose. But some things no ordering of sentences can fix: a reader who has to hold too much in their head, or see something words can't show. That is where a figure earns its place. So write the prose first, find where a reader would have to juggle too much, and add a figure only there. This page's two main figures each answer one such failure. The two orders are drawn one above the other, because comparing a paragraph with its rewrite from memory is a memory test. Amit Patel, who makes the interactive explanations at Red Blob Games, puts it as a rule: \"It's often better to show all the variants than to ask the reader to interact with the diagram to see the variants.\" The beat switches are interactive for the opposite reason: there are more variants (one per switch, and their combinations) than a page can show, and the claim that each beat matters is only convincing once you have removed one yourself. Three rules make an interactive teach rather than decorate: The default state already teaches. Patel: \"when feasible, start a diagram in a 'useful' state that doesn't require interaction to learn from it.\" The beat switches start with every beat on, so a reader who never touches them, or has no JavaScript, still reads the whole rewrite. Say what to do and what to look for in the sentence before it. \"Switch off the crack and read the fix again\" is an instruction; \"explore the demo\" is not. The caption says what to notice , not what the picture is, and what not to read into it. The beat switches' caption says their notes are a reading, not a measurement."},{"id":"practice","heading":"Your turn: a drafting routine","text":"Here is the whole method as a routine for your next explanation: a blog post, a design doc, an onboarding page. Name the reader and the one question the page answers, with a number in it if you can. Leaving out fundamentals that reader already has is correct, not careless. Read two or three existing explanations and mark where you stalled. Whichever step stalled you gets a section of its own in your page. Note the wrong model your reader most likely brings. Pick one running example that keeps the hard part hard, from real data or real code where you can. Lay out the ladder: for each idea a need, a naive attempt, a crack and a fix, joined by \"but\" and \"therefore\". List your promises: every simplification and deferral, with where it is paid off. Place one or two predictions at the key step, built so the nearest wrong model gives a different answer. Draft the prose , add each figure against a named failure of the prose, and write the opening and the summary last. Get a cold read. Ask someone who doesn't know the topic where they first stalled, not what they thought of it. The last step is the one the curse of knowledge makes necessary, because you can't read your own page as a newcomer. Ask the reader to read silently and stop at the first sentence where they lost the thread. When no person is free, this blog uses a fresh AI agent as a stand-in: given only the page and the reader's background, with no conversation history, it reports its first stall, every term it couldn't place, and every number whose origin it couldn't trace. It has read far more than any newcomer, so treat what it finds as a minimum, not the whole list. Optional exercise: rewrite a clear paragraph about B-trees Here is an explanation in the same textbook shape as Wikipedia's. I wrote it for this exercise, but you will find versions of it in many books: “A B-tree is a balanced search tree in which each node holds many keys and has many children. Its large fanout keeps the tree shallow, which minimises the number of disk reads per lookup.” It is clear and correct. Rewrite it with a need, a naive attempt, a crack and a fix, and keep the word \"fanout\" until the end. Open the hints one at a time, only when you are stuck. Hint 1: what would a reader try first? The need: look a key up among a billion stored on disk, where each read from disk costs far more than any comparison. What search structure would a programmer reach for first? Hint 2: count what that attempt costs A balanced binary search tree over a billion keys (about 2 30 ) is about 30 levels deep, and each level is a separate read from disk. Each read also fetches a whole page, typically 4 to 16 KB, only to use one key from it. Where is the waste, and what property must the fix have? Hint 3: one worked answer Need: a lookup among a billion keys on disk, where each read costs far more than any comparison. Naive attempt: a balanced binary search tree. Crack: about 30 reads per lookup, and each read brings in a whole page to use one key, so the fix must use the rest of the page. Fix: fill each node with as many keys as a page holds, a few hundred, so one read narrows the search a few-hundred-fold, and a billion keys need about 4 levels. Name: the number of children per node is the fanout, and a tree built this way is a B-tree."},{"id":"limits","heading":"When the loop is the wrong tool","text":"Everything so far assumes a reader who has to learn the idea. But the loop spends words to buy understanding, and not every reader wants to buy it. Reference material. A reader looking up a field width wants the table, not a story. Put specifications, full tables and edge cases after the narrative or on a page of their own, as this blog does with the DEFLATE format reference . Readers who need the decision, not the idea. Someone approving a design wants the proposal first. This blog's design-doc template puts the proposed design before the alternatives for that reason. Even then, each section should open by saying where it is going. Readers who already know. Help that a newcomer needs slows an expert down, measurably; the research calls it the expertise reversal effect (Kalyuga and colleagues, 2003). Give experts a visible way past the scaffolding, as the line under this page's summary does. Wrong models your reader doesn't hold. Refuting a misconception works when the reader holds it. Refuting one they don't is a detour. Bartosz Ciechanowski's Airfoil builds the right model of lift and leaves the popular wrong explanations to its further reading. Interesting extras. Anecdotes and history added as colour compete with the idea for the reader's attention. Muller and Sharma tested this too: an \"Extended Exposition\" with \"additional interesting information\" did no better than the plain lecture. Put extras in a sidenote, or after the payoff. Your own aha. The analogy that finally made an idea click for you is the residue of hours spent with its details. Handed over without the details, it explains nothing. Brent Yorgey named this the monad tutorial fallacy: \"failing to recognize the critical role that struggling through fundamental details plays in the building of intuition.\" Give the details and examples, and the analogy at the end, as a summary. A toy that teaches the wrong thing. Sanderson traced much of the confusion over his video on Grover's algorithm to an example. \"This is almost certainly exacerbated by me briefly flashing an example function that just checks if the input is 12\", which made the function look as if it already knew the answer. Ask what a literal reader would infer from your example."},{"id":"answer","heading":"Back to the book on the ramp","text":"The question was what to change in a clear, correct explanation that a reader feels good about and cannot use. The student on the ramp had a model before the video started: something has to win for the book to keep moving. The clear video never mentioned that model, so nothing in it collided with it. She heard true sentences, fitted them around what she already believed, and felt she understood better. The Dialogue said her model out loud and broke it on a case. That is a naive attempt and a crack, and it doubled what students learned. So what we change is not the clarity. We change the order, and we ask for commitment. Give the reader the question before the answer and their own wrong answer before the right one; break the wrong answer where the break points at the fix; name the fix last; then ask a question their old model gets wrong. The Wikipedia paragraph needed nothing new for any of this except a count, the reader's own wrong answer, a check and a new order. Here is the whole method in five lines, the four beats and the check: Need: a question, with numbers, that the reader wants answered. Naive attempt: the obvious answer, tried concretely and labelled as a first try. Crack: where it breaks, phrased as the property any fix must have. Fix, then name: the fix in the words of that property, then its technical name. Check: a question the reader's old model answers differently. Nothing here is specific to prose. This blog's template for decision records runs the loop by construction: the context is the need, each option considered is an attempt with its drawbacks as its crack, and the decision is the fix. So does a review comment that shows the failing input before the suggested change, or a commit message that says what broke before what changed. Reach for the loop when you catch yourself writing the answer first: a definition, a rule, a \"simply\". Reach for a check whenever a reader tells you an explanation was clear. As the ramp showed, that is the one thing a reader can say whether or not they learned. Two cautions on how far to trust all this. The evidence comes mostly from short lessons with novices, physics students in the clearest case, tested soon afterwards; the effects are moderate, not miracles. And the core of the method comes from one explainer's habits, Grant Sanderson's, found by counting simple features across 118 of his scripts (sentence length, how often he says \"you\", how often he asks the viewer to predict) and cross-checked against other writers and the research. Treat it as a strong default, and check it against your own readers."},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"The pedagogical-writing skill , in this blog's repository: the full set of moves this page condenses, distilled from 245 3Blue1Brown videos and from other explainers, writing guides and learning research. Its sources file grades each source by the strength of its evidence. Derek Muller and Manjula Sharma, “Tackling misconceptions in introductory physics using multimedia presentations” (UniServe Science proceedings, 2007): the ramp student, the confidence data and the Dialogue, in six readable pages. Christoph Kulgemeyer and Jörg Wittwer, “Misconceptions in Physics Explainer Videos and the Illusion of Understanding” (International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education, 2022): a clear but wrong video felt as convincing as a correct one. Kyle St. Hilaire, Jason Chan and Dahwi Ahn, “Guessing as a learning intervention: a meta-analytic review of the prequestion effect” (Psychonomic Bulletin & Review, 2024): why the predictions on this page ask you to commit. Louis Alfieri and colleagues, “Does discovery-based instruction enhance learning?” (Journal of Educational Psychology, 2011): unassisted discovery against guided discovery. Grant Sanderson (3Blue1Brown), “What makes a great math explanation?” and “Where my explanation of Grover's algorithm failed” : an explainer on his own method, and an honest postmortem of a toy that taught the wrong thing. The other quotes are from “Make math videos!” , “There's never been a better time to go into math” and “But what is quantum computing?” . George Gopen and Judith Swan, “The Science of Scientific Writing” (American Scientist, 1990): topic and stress positions, with worked rewrites. Steven Pinker, The Sense of Style (2014), chapter 3: the curse of knowledge, and why rereading your own prose can't find it. Nicky Case, “How I make an explorable explanation” : BUT and THEREFORE, and a playground built one piece at a time. Amit Patel, “Little design elements” : dozens of small choices that make interactive diagrams teach, including the two quoted here. Brent Yorgey, “Abstraction, intuition, and the ‘monad tutorial fallacy’” : why your analogy is not your reader's explanation. Wikipedia, “Dynamic array” : the paragraph examined here, and a table of the growth factors real languages use."}]},{"href":"docs/token-bucket-rate-limiting.html","title":"Token bucket rate limiting","kind":"explainer","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Rate limiting Token bucket rate limiting How one small data structure lets clients burst when they've been quiet, while still holding them to an average rate — and the production details that the textbook version leaves out. A token bucket lets a client that has been quiet send a burst, and still holds it to an average rate. It needs two numbers per client and no timer. Its two knobs mean something you can choose from your capacity: one sets the largest burst, the other the sustained rate. The common mistake is counting requests in fixed windows. It lets twice the limit through at the seam between two windows, and no setting fixes that."},{"id":"the-need","heading":"What a rate limiter has to allow","text":"Suppose 200 clients share a backend that serves 2,000 requests per second. Split evenly, each client gets 10 per second . Two of them misbehave in different ways. The dashboard Loading a page fires 25 calls in parallel, then nothing for 30 seconds. That averages under 1 request per second, far below its share of 10. The retry loop A script stuck in a loop sends 500 per second for as long as it runs. That is 50 times its share. A limiter has to admit the dashboard's 25 calls and refuse almost all of the script's 500. That is two demands at once: cap the average at 10 per second, and don't punish a short burst from a client that has been quiet. Let's see how far the obvious rules get."},{"id":"fixed-window","heading":"A first try: count requests per second","text":"The obvious rule is \"at most 10 requests per second\". Keep a counter per client, reset it to zero every second, and refuse a request when the counter is already 10. The state is tiny: a count and the time the window started. Limit: 10 per second, counted in fixed one-second windows. What is the most requests one client can get admitted within a single second? Decide before you open this. 20. The limit only holds inside each window, so a client can spend all 10 at the end of one window and 10 more at the start of the next. Anyone who answered 10 assumed the limit holds for any second you pick. It holds only for the seconds the counter happens to start and stop on. 0.95 s 10 requests arrive The counter for the window 0 to 1 s reaches 10. All are admitted. 1.00 s The counter resets A new window begins at 0. 1.05 s 10 more arrive All are admitted. That is 20 requests in 0.1 seconds, against a limit of 10 per second. That is the first crack, and it comes from the reset. The counter forgets a burst at an arbitrary instant, so a burst that straddles the reset is invisible. The second crack is the dashboard. Its 25 calls arrive in one window, so 15 are refused, even though the client has used nothing in the last 30 seconds. Quiet earns nothing. Both come from judging a request by which clock second it landed in . A better rule would judge it by how much the client has used lately ."},{"id":"sliding-log","heading":"A second try: remember every request","text":"Then do that. Keep the timestamp of every admitted request and admit a new one only if fewer than 10 fall in the last second. There is no seam to hide a burst in: any one-second span holds at most 10. It costs memory. Ten timestamps of 8 bytes is 80 bytes per client, which is 80 MB for a million clients. The cost grows with the limit: at 1,000 per second it is 8 KB per client, or 8 GB. And the dashboard still loses 15 of its 25 calls, because \"10 in the last second\" is as flat as \"10 per window\". Quiet still earns nothing. So the rule we want has two properties the first two tries lack. Unused allowance should build up , up to a cap. And its state should not grow with the limit."},{"id":"mental-model","heading":"The fix: save up what you don't use","text":"Both tries ask how much a client has used . Ask the opposite: how much does it have left to spend ? Picture a bucket under a dripping tap. Each drip is permission for one request. A request may pass only if it can take a drip out. If the bucket is full, the extra drips spill away and are lost. That spill is the whole design. Saving is capped, so a client that has been idle for an hour can't then fire an hour's worth of requests at once. The token bucket is equivalent to GCRA (the generic cell rate algorithm) from ATM networking. GCRA stores one timestamp instead of a token count and a timestamp. Two numbers describe a bucket. Its capacity b is how many tokens it can hold, which is the largest burst. The tap's rate r is how many tokens it adds per second, which is the sustained rate. A client that has been idle for b/r seconds, the time to fill from empty, starts with a full bucket. Here is the picture as a machine you can work. Each coin is a token. The magazine is the bucket, the dial on top is the tap, and the button sends a request. A coin that reaches the service bin was admitted. A coin dropped onto a full magazine spills into the lost bin, and when the magazine is empty the 429 flag goes up. Fig 1 Token bucket Press the button or Space 6 of 6 tokens · 0 admitted · 0 rejected A coin on the move: dropped by the timer, sent on to the service, or lost The six coins the magazine holds A toy bucket that holds six tokens (b = 6), refilled by one every 1.25 s (r = 0.8 per second). Each press takes the bottom coin; with none left the pusher finds nothing and the 429 flag goes up. A coin dropped onto a full magazine is lost. The machine counts whole coins and its drip clock keeps running when the magazine is full, so after a take from a full magazine the next coin can land sooner than the refill rule below allows. This is a token bucket , and the numbers it needs are the ones the story asked for. Give each client b = 25 and r = 10 . The dashboard finds a full bucket, so all 25 calls are admitted and the bucket empties. The retry loop gets its first 25, then one request per tap drip: 10 a second, not 500. In its first second, 35 of its 500 get through: the 25 in the full bucket plus the 10 that dripped in. And there is no seam to hide in, because nothing resets. The state is two numbers per client: the tokens left, and the time of the last visit. Two 8-byte numbers make 16 bytes, which is 16 MB for a million clients, whatever the limit is."},{"id":"no-timer","heading":"But who turns the tap?","text":"A real limiter can't run this machine as it stands. With a million clients it would need a million taps, each dripping 10 times a second: 10 million timer events every second, nearly all for clients that are idle. Nobody looks at a bucket between requests, so nothing needs to drip then. When a request arrives, work out what the tap would have delivered since the last visit, add it, and cap the total at b . The machine above is the picture. In code there is no clock: the drips are worked out from the time that has passed whenever a request arrives."},{"id":"how-it-works","heading":"How a request is decided","text":"Everything now happens at the moment a request arrives. The diagram shows the pieces, and the steps beneath it walk through one request. The limiter is the only part that acts. In code, refill isn't a background process. It is calculated when a request arrives. A request arrives at the limiter with a timestamp now . Refill lazily. Add (now − last) × r tokens and cap the total at b . Anything over the cap spills. Store last = now . Try to take a token. If tokens ≥ 1 , subtract one. A request can cost more than one token. Weight expensive endpoints that way. Admitted. Forward the request. The state for this client is just two numbers: tokens and last . Rejected. Return 429 with Retry-After = (1 − tokens) / r , the seconds until one whole token exists. The client knows exactly how long to wait, so it doesn't need to guess with a backoff."},{"id":"simulation","heading":"Watch it run","text":"Two claims to check: b sets the largest burst, and r sets the sustained rate. The slots show tokens in the bucket, and the partly filled slot is the next token arriving. Beside them, on the right, are the last ten seconds of requests. Set the background traffic, then press Burst ×10 and watch the bucket drain. Then lower r and press it again. Capacity b Refill rate r (tokens/s) Background traffic (req/s) Send 1 Burst ×10 Reset Tokens 0 · admitted 0 · rejected 0 · refill from empty takes s b = 10, r = 1/s, bucket full. A client sends 15 requests at once, then 1 per second. What happens? The first 10 are admitted and the next 5 are rejected. After that, every request at 1 per second is admitted. Each one spends the single token that arrived in the second before it, so the bucket stays almost empty. The client can't burst again until it slows below r and lets tokens build up."},{"id":"choosing-parameters","heading":"Choosing b and r","text":"The simulation shows what the knobs do. The next question is what to set them to. Start from what the downstream system can take, not from what clients would like to send. r The sustained rate you can serve for every client at once, with headroom. The 200 clients sharing a backend of 2,000 req/s each get r = 10, the same 10 per second the first try tried to enforce. b The largest legitimate burst, such as the dashboard's 25 parallel calls. Too small and real users hit 429s. Too large and one client can saturate a replica briefly. A bucket that has just emptied takes b/r = 2.5 s to fill again. cost Requests don't all weigh the same. Charge a report export 20 tokens and a health check 0."},{"id":"implementation","heading":"Implementation","text":"Now the code for the refill-then-take step. The core is about ten lines. The distributed version moves the same arithmetic into Redis, so the read-modify-write is atomic. use std::time::{Duration, Instant}; pub struct TokenBucket { capacity: f64, // b — largest burst rate: f64, // r — tokens per second tokens: f64, last: Instant, // monotonic clock: immune to NTP jumps } impl TokenBucket { pub fn new(capacity: f64, rate: f64) -> Self { Self { capacity, rate, tokens: capacity, last: Instant::now() } } /// Ok(()) if admitted, otherwise Err(how long until `cost` tokens exist). pub fn try_acquire(&mut self, cost: f64) -> Result<(), Duration> { let now = Instant::now(); let elapsed = now.duration_since(self.last).as_secs_f64(); self.tokens = (self.tokens + elapsed * self.rate).min(self.capacity); self.last = now; if self.tokens >= cost { self.tokens -= cost; Ok(()) } else { Err(Duration::from_secs_f64((cost - self.tokens) / self.rate)) } } } type Bucket struct { mu sync.Mutex capacity float64 // b rate float64 // r, tokens per second tokens float64 last time.Time } func (b *Bucket) TryAcquire(cost float64) (ok bool, retryAfter time.Duration) { b.mu.Lock() defer b.mu.Unlock() now := time.Now() // carries a monotonic reading; Sub() uses it b.tokens = math.Min(b.capacity, b.tokens+now.Sub(b.last).Seconds()*b.rate) b.last = now if b.tokens >= cost { b.tokens -= cost return true, 0 } return false, time.Duration((cost - b.tokens) / b.rate * float64(time.Second)) } -- KEYS[1] = bucket key -- ARGV = capacity, rate (tokens/s), now (ms, from the Redis TIME command), cost local b, r, now, cost = tonumber(ARGV[1]), tonumber(ARGV[2]), tonumber(ARGV[3]), tonumber(ARGV[4]) local s = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts') local tokens = tonumber(s[1]) or b local ts = tonumber(s[2]) or now tokens = math.min(b, tokens + math.max(0, now - ts) / 1000 * r) local ok = tokens >= cost if ok then tokens = tokens - cost end redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now) redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(b / r * 1000) + 1000) -- idle keys vanish when full return { ok and 1 or 0, tostring(tokens) }"},{"id":"alternatives","heading":"How it compares","text":"The two failed tries above are in this table as fixed window and sliding log. The rest are the usual alternatives. Algorithm Bursts State per key Boundary spikes Best for Token bucket Up to b 2 numbers None APIs: tolerate bursts, enforce an average Leaky bucket (queue) Smoothed queue None Shaping output to a fixed rate Fixed window Up to 2× at edges 1 counter Yes Coarse quotas (per day) Sliding log Exact N timestamps None Low volume, exact limits Sliding window counter Approximate 2 counters Small A cheap approximation of the log"},{"id":"gotchas","heading":"Gotchas in production","text":"The arithmetic is the easy part. In production the same ten lines go wrong in three ways. Wall-clock time Measure elapsed time with a monotonic clock. If NTP steps the wall clock backwards, the elapsed time comes out negative. Without a clamp, that can drain the bucket, or in some code mint a huge pile of tokens. Per-node limits multiply Ten gateway replicas, each with its own bucket at r = 100, give a client an effective limit of 1,000. Either share state (the Redis script above) or divide r by the replica count and accept some unfairness. Read-modify-write races A distributed limiter that does GET and then SET as two round trips lets concurrent requests spend the same token twice. Run the update atomically, with a Lua script or a database transaction. Go deeper: why return Retry-After? Clients that get a bare 429 back off blindly, usually with exponential backoff, and often all at the same moment. You already know when the next token will exist, so tell them. That turns a stampede of retries into an orderly queue."},{"id":"back-to-the-clients","heading":"Back to the two clients","text":"The question was how to admit the dashboard's 25 calls and refuse the retry loop's 500 a second. A bucket with b = 25 and r = 10 admits all 25 of the dashboard's, and holds the loop to 10 a second after its first 25. Next time you need a limit, ask two things. Should a quiet client be allowed to burst? And can you afford state that grows with the limit? If the first is yes and the second no, you want a token bucket."},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"Token bucket (Wikipedia) : the formal definition and its relation to the leaky bucket. Generic cell rate algorithm : the same idea, storing a single timestamp. RFC 9110 §10.2.3, Retry-After : the header semantics."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#adr","title":"ADR","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"ADRs, architecture decision record, architecture decision records","text":"An architecture decision record: a short, permanent note of one significant decision, covering its context, the options considered and the consequences. ADRs are superseded by new records rather than edited."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#back-reference","title":"Back-reference","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"back-references, backreference, backreferences","text":"In LZ77 compression, an instruction to copy L bytes starting D bytes back in the output already produced, instead of storing them again. DEFLATE allows distances up to 32,768 bytes. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#backoff","title":"Backoff","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"exponential backoff","text":"Waiting longer after each failed attempt before retrying, usually doubling the delay each time. Without jitter , clients that failed together retry together."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#barrier","title":"Barrier","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"barriers","text":"A GPU instruction that makes every thread in a threadgroup wait until all of them arrive, and makes their earlier memory writes visible to each other. There is no safe barrier across threadgroups."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#burst","title":"Burst","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"bursts","text":"A short spike of requests above the average rate. In a token bucket, the largest burst allowed is the bucket's capacity b ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#cache-miss","title":"Cache miss","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"cache misses","text":"A lookup that finds nothing in the cache, so the request falls through to the slower source of truth and usually back-fills the cache."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#canonical-huffman-code","title":"Canonical Huffman code","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"canonical Huffman codes, canonical code, canonical codes","text":"A Huffman code rebuilt from code lengths alone: symbols are sorted by length, then by value, and each gets the previous code plus one, shifted left when the length grows. A DEFLATE block sends only the lengths. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#compute-kernel","title":"Compute kernel","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"compute kernels, kernel, kernels, compute shader, compute shaders","text":"A function that runs on the GPU once per thread of a dispatch . Each thread reads its own index to decide which part of the work it does."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#crc-32","title":"CRC-32","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"CRC, CRCs, CRC32","text":"A 32-bit checksum computed by polynomial division over the bits of the data. ZIP and gzip store one per file so a decoder can detect corruption. Checksums of adjacent pieces can be combined, which allows computing it in parallel. Cyclic redundancy check (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#deflate","title":"DEFLATE","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"The compression format inside ZIP, gzip and PNG: LZ77 back-references plus Huffman coding , organised in blocks. Its bit stream has no index, so decoding is serial. Step by step · RFC 1951"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#dispatch","title":"Dispatch","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"dispatches","text":"One launch of a compute kernel over a grid of threads, divided into threadgroups . Dispatches in one command buffer run in order, which is the safe way to order work between threadgroups."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#divergence","title":"Divergence","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"divergent, diverge, diverges","text":"When lanes of one SIMD group take different sides of a branch. The hardware runs each side in turn with the other lanes switched off, so the branch costs the sum of both sides."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#end-of-block","title":"End-of-block","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"end of block, end-of-block symbol, end-of-block code","text":"Symbol 256 of DEFLATE 's literal/length alphabet. It closes every Huffman-coded block, so it needs a code of its own even though it appears once. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#extra-bits","title":"Extra bits","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"extra bit, length extra bits, distance extra bits","text":"In DEFLATE , the plain bits that follow a length or distance symbol to pick an exact value, because each symbol stands for a range: symbol 270 means a length from 23 to 26, and the 2 extra bits after it say which. They are written as a plain number, not Huffman-coded, and the symbol alone decides how many there are. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#gcra","title":"GCRA","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"generic cell rate algorithm","text":"The generic cell rate algorithm. It is equivalent to a token bucket, but stores one timestamp per key: the theoretical arrival time of the next conforming request. Generic cell rate algorithm (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#huffman-code","title":"Huffman code","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"Huffman codes, Huffman coding, Huffman table, Huffman tables","text":"A variable-length binary code that gives frequent symbols short bit patterns and rare ones long patterns. No code is a prefix of another, so a decoder can read them back to back without separators. Step by step · Huffman coding (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#jitter","title":"Jitter","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Randomness added to retry or polling delays, so many clients that start together don't keep acting in lock-step and overload a recovering service."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#lane","title":"Lane","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"lanes","text":"One thread's slot within a SIMD group . Each lane has its own registers, but all lanes execute the same instruction at the same time."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#lcov","title":"lcov","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"lcov report, lcov reports","text":"A plain-text coverage format that lists, per source file, how many times each line ran. Most coverage tools can write it, which makes it the common currency between them and tools like patchcov."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#leaky-bucket","title":"Leaky bucket","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"leaky buckets","text":"A queue that drains at a fixed rate. It smooths output to a constant pace instead of allowing bursts; compare with a token bucket ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#lz77","title":"LZ77","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"The compression idea, from Lempel and Ziv in 1977, of replacing repeated data with back-references to an earlier occurrence. Step by step · LZ77 and LZ78 (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#marker","title":"Marker","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"markers","text":"In rapidgzip-style decoding, a 16-bit placeholder meaning \"the byte n positions before this chunk started\". It lets a decoder start mid-stream without the history, and is replaced by the real byte once the previous chunk is decoded."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#merge-base","title":"Merge base","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"merge bases, fork point, fork points","text":"The newest commit that a branch and its target (usually main ) share: where the branch forked. Comparing against it, rather than the tip of main , shows only what the branch itself changed."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#monotonic-clock","title":"Monotonic clock","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"monotonic clocks","text":"A clock that only moves forwards. Use it to measure elapsed time, because the wall clock can jump backwards when NTP corrects it."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#occupancy","title":"Occupancy","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"How many SIMD groups a GPU core keeps resident at once. More resident groups give the core more work to switch to while others wait for memory. Registers and threadgroup memory per group limit it."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#p99","title":"p99","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"99th percentile","text":"The 99th-percentile latency: 99% of requests complete at least this fast. Tail latency like this, rather than the average, is what users notice."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#patch-coverage","title":"Patch coverage","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"diff coverage","text":"The share of the executable lines a change added that the tests ran. Unlike the project-wide total, it shows the gaps in new code even when they are too small to move the total. Announcing patchcov lets you try it."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#rate-limiting","title":"Rate limiting","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"rate limit, rate limits, rate limiter, rate limiters","text":"Capping how many requests a client may make in a period of time, to protect a service and share its capacity fairly. Token bucket rate limiting explains the most common algorithm."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#read-replica","title":"Read replica","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"read replicas","text":"A copy of a database that serves reads and follows the primary asynchronously, so it can lag slightly behind."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#retry-after","title":"Retry-After","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"An HTTP response header that tells the client how long to wait before retrying, sent with 429 or 503 . RFC 9110 §10.2.3"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#retry-budget","title":"Retry budget","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"retry budgets","text":"A cap on retries as a fraction of normal traffic (for example 10%). It stops retries from multiplying load during an outage."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#shuffle","title":"Shuffle","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"shuffles, lane shuffle, lane shuffles","text":"A GPU instruction in which every lane reads a register value from another lane of its SIMD group, without going through memory. In Metal it is simd_shuffle ; in HLSL, WaveReadLaneAt ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#simd-group","title":"SIMD group","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"SIMD groups, simdgroup, simdgroups, warp, warps, subgroup, subgroups, wavefront, wavefronts","text":"The set of threads a GPU executes in lock-step: 32 on Apple GPUs (NVIDIA calls it a warp, Vulkan a subgroup, HLSL a wave). One instruction is fetched once and executed by every lane ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#sliding-window","title":"Sliding window","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"sliding windows","text":"In LZ77 , the most recent bytes already processed, which back-references may copy from. It moves forward with the input; DEFLATE 's is 32,768 bytes. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#slo","title":"SLO","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"SLOs, service level objective, service level objectives","text":"A service level objective: a reliability target, such as 99.9% of requests succeeding over 30 days. It is set below 100% on purpose, leaving an error budget."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#spir-v","title":"SPIR-V","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"The Khronos binary intermediate language for GPU programs. Vulkan drivers consume it directly, and SPIRV-Cross can translate it into Metal Shading Language. SPIR-V registry"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#threadgroup","title":"Threadgroup","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"threadgroups, workgroup, workgroups, thread block, thread blocks","text":"A group of GPU threads that runs on one core and can share threadgroup memory and barriers . Vulkan calls it a workgroup and CUDA a thread block."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#threadgroup-memory","title":"Threadgroup memory","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"groupshared, groupshared memory, shared memory, workgroup memory","text":"Small, fast on-chip memory shared by the threads of one threadgroup : at most 32 KiB per threadgroup on Apple GPUs. HLSL calls it groupshared ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#tls","title":"TLS","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"transport layer security","text":"Transport Layer Security: the protocol that encrypts and authenticates a connection, as in HTTPS. A gateway that \"terminates TLS\" decrypts traffic there."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#token-bucket","title":"Token bucket","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"token buckets","text":"A rate limiter that refills tokens at rate r up to a capacity b , and admits each request only by spending a token. It allows bursts up to b but enforces r on average. Token bucket rate limiting"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#unified-memory","title":"Unified memory","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"A design, used by Apple Silicon, in which the CPU and GPU share the same physical DRAM. A buffer can be visible to both without being copied, though first GPU use of a buffer still has a cost."}]}];
